# The Terminal Widget — the Console Hosts the Intelligence Itself [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

> `intelligence/launcher/launcher.py` exists to make an Intelligence's `/compact` SELF-DIRECTED: it owns
> the child's PTY and types `/compact <direction>` when the instance emits a directive. It is a
> TRANSPARENT byte proxy today for one reason only — Claude Code's TUI needs somewhere to render and a
> human to answer the startup dialogs, and the only "somewhere" this platform had was a real terminal.
> That borrowed surface is macOS-only, un-styleable, un-inspectable, and not bus-native — the same
> category [CXC-RM-CTK-RETIRE] already retired when it replaced OS modals with in-engine affordances.
> This spec builds the durable surface instead: a ComposeUX widget that hosts the PTY, renders the real
> TUI, and takes the human's keystrokes. The load-bearing consequence is that a keystroke arriving from
> the BUS (the launcher's existing `inject` command) and a keystroke arriving from the KEYBOARD converge
> on the same fd — control-is-a-peer, completed at the input plane. V1 is deliberately narrow, and its
> FIRST task is a measurement, not a build.

---

## 1. The shape, and the two alternatives that were ruled out first

### [CXC-TERM-THESIS] The Console must be able to host an Intelligence with NO foreign surface

- premise: David — "My strong preference is for a Terminal Widget, but for V1 to be more narrowly scoped
  than the current mode I'm using (`/tui fullscreen`)."
- requirement: a ComposeApp MUST be able to bring an interactive `claude` session into existence, render
  its TUI, and accept the human's keystrokes, WITHOUT Terminal.app, without `osascript`, and without any
  macOS-only API — so the same scene runs on the macOS console, on a Raspberry Pi, and (after
  [CXC-RM-M3]) in a browser.
- rationale: the harness is the platform's most load-bearing lifecycle behaviour, and it currently rests
  on a surface this platform does not own, cannot style, cannot pixel-gate, and cannot inspect. Every
  other OS-owned surface in this system has already been retired for exactly those reasons
  ([CXC-RM-CTK-RETIRE]: "OS modals don't exist on CYD/browser, break the pixel gate, and aren't
  bus-native"). A Terminal dependency is the same category, one layer up.
- consequence: **(the thing that makes it more than a port)** once the PTY master is owned by a
  widget in
  the console process, the launcher's `inject` actuation and the human's keyboard write to the SAME fd
  through the same lock. A bus message and a keypress become indistinguishable to the child — see
  [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE].
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-SCOPE], [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-REJECT-TERMINAL] REJECTED — `osascript` / Terminal.app spawn (it works, and it is still a foreign surface)

- decision: rejected as the DURABLE mechanism. It ships in [CXC-ICL-TERMINAL] as the bridge, and this
  spec retires it ([CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55]).
- evidence: **(why it cannot be the durable answer)**

| # | Objection | Detail |
|---|-----------|--------|
| 1 | macOS-only | `_terminal_argv` returns `None` on every non-darwin platform; the capability simply does not exist off macOS |
| 2 | un-styleable | the window is Terminal.app's; the console cannot theme it, place it, or make it part of a composition |
| 3 | un-inspectable | the console cannot read what the child rendered, so no pixel gate, no capture-diff, no headless test |
| 4 | not bus-native | nothing about the session is on the bus; every other console surface is |
- constraint: objection 5 is the tell. The workaround is sound and specced, but a mechanism that needs a
  command-injection guard to pass a single identifier is a mechanism at the wrong layer. A widget passes
  the id as a Python argument.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-REJECT-HEADLESS] DISQUALIFIED — `--print --input-format stream-json --output-format stream-json` cannot express `/compact`

- decision: DISQUALIFIED, permanently, not deferred.
- evidence: `/compact` is NOT among the slash commands supported in `-p` mode. The documented supported
  set is `/config`, `/effort`, `/fast`, `/model`, `/color`, `/mcp`, `/status`, `/tasks`, `/usage`.
  Confirmed against the Claude Code documentation.
- rationale: self-directed compaction is the entire reason the harness exists ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]). A
  hosting mode that structurally cannot express it cannot host an Intelligence — it can only run a task.
  This is a capability gap, not a polish gap, so no amount of implementation effort closes it.
- constraint: this disqualification MUST NOT be re-litigated by pointing at the stream-json protocol's
  ergonomics. The protocol is nicer; it is also unable to do the one thing required.
- known-hazard: **(the tempting partial)** `-p` would make rendering trivial (no escape sequences at
  all),
  which is exactly why it will be re-proposed. Recorded here so the answer is one lookup away.
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-SCOPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-FACTS] Three facts from the same investigation the builder MUST carry

- definition: the headless investigation produced three findings that outlive its rejection.

| # | Fact | Why it matters here |
|---|------|---------------------|
| 1 | The session transcript `.jsonl` IS still written in ALL modes | The console data layer reads that transcript ([CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY], `data_sources.resolve_session_jsonl`). Hosting under a widget does NOT put it at risk — this is acceptance TERM-C7, and it is expected to pass on the first try |
| 2 | `--bg` cannot be combined with `-p` | Kills the "run it headless in the background and stream it into a widget" hybrid before it is proposed |
| 3 | `--remote-control` steers a session from claude.ai / mobile | It is a HUMAN remote, not a programmatic hosting API. Not a candidate |
- consequence: fact 1 is why acceptance criterion TERM-C7 is a REGRESSION check rather than a design
  risk. The widget must still export the session pin before fork ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]) — the transcript
  is written regardless, but the console must know WHICH one.
- correction: **(FIRST LIVE GLASS CHECK, 2026-08-11 — fact 1 held, but its PREMISE did not)** the
  transcript is written in all modes ONLY if the child is a top-level session. The first live spawn
  it was a NESTED child session, and TRANSCRIPT SAVING WAS OFF. TERM-C7 therefore had a hidden
  precondition — the child must MINT ITS OWN IDENTITY — which is now enforced in the spawn itself
  ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-IDENTITY]).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-CHILD-IDENTITY] The spawned instance MINTS its own identity — the child env is SANITISED, never inherited whole

- premise: **(measured on the live child, 2026-08-11 — the first glass check of this widget)**
  `pty.fork()` + a raw `os.environ` copy handed the hosted instance its HOST's identity, verbatim.

  | | value |
  |---|---|
- constraint: **(why this is STRUCTURAL, not an artifact of one launch)** this widget's whole
  purpose is
  "the console hosts the Intelligence", so its INTENDED deployment is FROM INSIDE a Claude Code
  session. It will inherit these ALWAYS, in production. There is no launch discipline that fixes it.
- consequence: **(two, the second worse)**
  1. transcript saving is OFF in the hosted instance — it believes it is a nested child session, which
     silently voids the precondition of [CXC-TERM-FACTS] fact 1 / TERM-C7;
     (`data_sources.resolve_session_jsonl` leg 2), so a hosted instance wearing its host's id resolves
     to the HOST'S TRANSCRIPT — the exact cross-instance leak that fix exists to prevent (the `ls -t`
     newest-wins defect, recurred 2026-07-24).
- requirement: `PtyChild.start()` MUST exec with a SANITISED environment (`sanitize_child_env`), never
  a raw copy. The strip list is DERIVED from what a spawned instance must MINT FOR ITSELF — identity,
  parentage, the in-harness flag, its IPC endpoint, its entrypoint, its pid, and its transcript pin —
  and every entry carries its own stated reason. Anything describing the MACHINE or the BINARY rather
  than the RUNNING SESSION is KEPT (`CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH` above all).
- requirement: **(PASSED, never INHERITED)** `env_extra` is applied AFTER the strip. That ordering
  is the
  mechanism: a RESUME/MOVE pin naming the CHILD's own transcript is passed back in explicitly, so the
  system can still tell a deliberate pin from a leaked one. Both hosts do this identically.
- requirement: **(the binary, same live check)** the child binary resolves
  `explicit -> $CLAUDE_BIN -> $CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH -> "claude"`. The bare-name default FAILED on the
  glass: `claude` is a shell ALIAS on this machine and is NOT on PATH, and a windowed host has no
  shell to expand it, so `execvp` exhausted PATH and died with a misleading "No such file" naming a
  stale plugin dir. `CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH` is harness-set, absolute, and names THIS claude.
- scenario:
  - when: the widget spawns a session from inside a running Claude Code session
    and no inherited transcript pin — it mints its own, saves its own transcript, and resolves its own
    session
- verify-machine: a unit test asserts the stripped keys are ABSENT and the kept ones PRESENT in the
  constructed env, AND a live `pty.fork()` + `execvpe` test dumps the real child's environment and
  asserts the host's id does not appear in it — the same way the defect was measured, because a unit
  test over the sanitiser alone would still pass if `start()` regained its `os.execvp` branch.
- verify-human: David confirms a widget-hosted instance writes its OWN transcript (the console's
  self/thread shows the hosted session, not the host's).
- upholds: [CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] ONE id, TWO instances — the hosted child carries an id DISTINCT from its host's

- premise: (measured, the FIRST LIVE SPAWN, 2026-08-11) the scene hardcodes
  `intelligence_id: architect` (`python/composeio/render/examples/terminal_session.compose.yaml`
  line 44) — the SAME id as the host Architect that spawned it. One cause, THREE reported
  symptoms:

| # | Symptom | Mechanism |
|---|---------|-----------|
| 1 | the child DISPLACED the host on the channel | `intelligence/channel/channel.mjs:485-490` — "Single-instance identity ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL]): if a NEWER bridge for this same id announces itself, THIS (older) bridge yields the identity and exits". The HOST's `channel.mjs` exited; the CHILD's survived. The host lost its voice to David's console |
| 2 | the IC showed the HOST's status | the console publishes `self/architect/*` off the HOST's transcript, and the child publishes no telemetry at all ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]) |
| 3 | the IC showed the HOST's transcript CONTENT | under a label that fits BOTH instances, so nothing on the glass said which of the two you were reading |

- requirement: (David's ruling, settled 2026-08-11 — UNCHANGED; only the derivation below is amended) a widget-hosted child MUST be spawned under
  an id DISTINCT from its host's. The scene no longer hands a single `intelligence_id` scalar
  through to the child verbatim: it carries the ROLE, and the id the child WEARS is the
  INSTANCE's ([DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME] records how it is minted).
- requirement: (a host-id collision is REFUSED, not warned) if the id the spawn would use
  equals the HOST's own `INTELLIGENCE_ID` (or the id of any launcher singleton this process
  already holds), the widget REFUSES before `pty.fork()` and says so on the glass. A warning is
  not enough because all three symptoms above are invisible FROM INSIDE the child — the child
  sees a working channel and a healthy transcript; only the displaced host and the human at the
  console see the damage, and by then the host is already off the air.
- requirement: (the child's namespaces follow the child's id) a hosted child's channel topics,
  its singleton lock name (`intelligence-launcher-<child-id>`, [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON]), its
  telemetry namespace (`self/<child-id>/*`, [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]) and its
  `terminal/degraded` publish ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]) ALL key on the CHILD's id. One id, one
  instance, everywhere — a mixed keying is symptom 2 in a new costume.
- consequence: (why this is the ROOT and the other two rulings are its consequences) symptom 1
  is closed twice over — here, by never handing out a duplicate id, and in
  [CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE], by refusing rather than displacing when a duplicate arrives anyway.
  Both are required: this one makes the collision rare; that one makes it AUDIBLE when the
  rarity fails.
- scenario:
  - when: the widget spawns a child while the console host is itself running as `architect`
  - then: the child runs under its own distinct id; the host's `channel.mjs` is still alive and still owns `intelligence/architect/{in,out}`
  - when: a scene is authored (or copy-pasted) naming the host's own id
  - then: no fork occurs, the refusal plate names both ids, and the widget stays re-launchable
  - when: the child's role resolves a ROLE manifest whose `id` is the role name, not the instance's
  - then: the child is still called by its INSTANCE id in every namespace including the bootstrap prompt (TERM-C19) — the role names the artifacts, never the peer
- verify-machine: a test spawns through the widget with `INTELLIGENCE_ID=architect` in the host
  env and asserts (a) the child's id differs, (b) the singleton lock name and the telemetry
  namespace both carry the CHILD's id, and (c) a scene naming the host id produces zero
  `pty.fork` calls and the refusal plate; a test asserts the host's channel bridge process is
  still alive after a child spawn.
- verify-human: David spawns a session in the widget and confirms the console still hears the
  HOST — his own Architect keeps answering on the channel — and that the IC's status and
  transcript for each instance are the instance's own.
- change: "2026-08-11, FIRST LIVE SPAWN: the hosted child is given an id DISTINCT from its host's. Previously the scene's `intelligence_id: architect` was carried through verbatim, so a widget-hosted child and the Architect hosting it were the same identity to the channel, to the telemetry namespace, and to the transcript resolver."
- context_for_change:
    reasoning: "Three defects were reported and there was one cause. `channel.mjs`'s displacement rule assumes same id implies same instance, `self_publisher --namespace-by-id` keys telemetry on the id, and the console labels a transcript by id — so a duplicated id is not one bug in three places, it is the single assumption every one of those mechanisms rests on. Fixing them individually would have produced three exceptions to three correct rules."
    invalidating_assumption: "That one instance is addressed by exactly one id. If instances ever need to be addressable BOTH individually and as a role group (a broadcast to every `architect`), the id stops being a single scalar and becomes an address plus a role membership — at which point [DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME] reopens and the channel needs a group-subscribe, not a second id."
- change: "2026-08-11 (fourth pass): the RULING is unchanged — a hosted child still carries an id distinct from its host's — and only its DERIVATION is amended. The ROLE the scene carries is now the BASENAME OF ITS ROLE DESCRIPTOR (`composeux-architect`, not the colliding bare `architect`), and the role/instance split is made explicit: the role selects the ARTIFACTS, the instance id is the NAME and wins wherever the two could differ. Its full context is [CXC-TERM-CHANGE-4-ROLE-NAME-AND-INSTANCE-ID] — a SECOND `context_for_change:` mapping on this node would silently overwrite the one above (measured with `lts_preview`; repeated `change:` keys make a list and survive, repeated block-valued keys do not)."
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-IDENTITY], [DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME]
- upholds: [CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN] Manifest resolution is a CHAIN — `<id>` then `<role>` then REFUSE, loudly and on the GLASS

- constraint: (why the chain is REQUIRED BY the ruling above, not a nicety beside it)
  `ptycore.default_meta_manifest` returns exactly ONE path,
  `intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json`. Give every hosted child a distinct id
  ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]) and that single leg MISSES for every child that is not hand-curated.
  Manifest-per-id cannot scale to N instances of one role, so without the chain the fix for the
  identity defect would have manufactured a fleet of unoriented instances.
- requirement: (the order is normative, and it is the same in BOTH hosts)
  1. `META_MANIFEST`, when explicitly set — an operator override, unchanged;
  2. `intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json` — a manifest curated for THIS instance;
  3. `intelligence/meta/<role>.meta.json` — the ROLE's manifest, which is what makes N
     instances of one role possible at all;
  4. REFUSE. No fork, no degraded boot, no silent orientation gap.
- requirement: (a leg-3 manifest is a ROLE artifact and its `id` NAMES THE ROLE, never the instance) a manifest resolved at leg 3 MUST NOT carry per-instance state — `window_geometry` and `console` are DROPPED, `channel.topics` states the SHAPE (`intelligence/<instance_id>/in`) with a `topics_note` and never a literal, and `lifecycle` values are the role's DEFAULTS with per-instance overrides written into a leg-2 manifest. A role manifest spelling the literal `intelligence/architect/in` would hand every instance of the role the HOST's own topics — the displacement defect of [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] arriving by a second route. The full key-by-key rule lives in `intelligence/meta/meta-object.schema.md`, which is the single source; this node states only what leg 3 depends on.
- requirement: (the manifest supplies ARTIFACTS; the INSTANCE supplies the NAME) resolving leg 3 MUST NOT change what the child is called. The prompt built from a role manifest names the RUNNING INSTANCE ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]'s instance-id-wins rule), because every other namespace the child touches — its channel topics, its `intelligence-launcher-<id>` lock, its `self/<id>/*` telemetry, its refusal publish — already keys on the instance id.
- known-hazard: (why this was invisible until tonight, and it is the general rule) leg 3 had NEVER ONCE been exercised with a real ROLE manifest. Only `architect.meta.json` existed — an INSTANCE manifest whose `id` happens to equal its role — so the manifest-derived name and the instance-derived name agreed BY COINCIDENCE, and the disagreement could not be observed. AN AGREEMENT THAT HOLDS BY COINCIDENCE IS NOT AN AGREEMENT, AND A LEG NEVER EXERCISED IS NOT A LEG THAT WORKS. Minting the first real role manifest is what exposed it; the chain test's role-hit leg MUST use a fixture whose `id` DIFFERS from the instance id, or it re-tests the coincidence.
- evidence: (what "logged only" actually means here, and why a log is not a surface) a child
  with no readable manifest currently reaches `build_bootstrap_for`, which returns
  `build_unmanifested_prompt`'s degraded text and calls `say(...)`. In the CLI host that line
  lands in a log file nobody watches; in the WIDGET host `terminal.py::_log` is literally
  `def _log(self, line: str) -> None: pass` — the diagnostic is discarded at the call site. An
  unoriented instance therefore looks IDENTICAL to an oriented one at the glass, which is the
  indistinguishability [SBR-TRUST] forbids one layer down ("'I could not load the warts' is a
  required output, never an empty section read as 'no warts'").
- requirement: (the refusal is LOUD and GLASS-VISIBLE) when the chain is exhausted the host
  MUST NOT fork. The widget draws a refusal plate naming the id, the role, and EVERY path it
  tried in order; it publishes the same payload to `intelligence/<child-id>/terminal/refusal`
  as `{"refused": true, "reason": "no-manifest", "id":…, "role":…, "paths_tried": [...]}` so a
  headless test and a monitor can see it too ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]'s bus-visible rule, applied to
  a refusal); and it stays immediately RE-LAUNCHABLE ([CXC-TERM-BOUNDARIES]).
- requirement: (the CLI host refuses identically) `launcher.py` gets the same chain and the same
  refusal — a non-zero exit naming the paths tried, not a log line and a launch. Two hosts with
  different answers to "what if there is no manifest" is the fork-divergence
  [CXC-TERM-PTYCORE] exists to prevent, and the divergent one is always the one nobody reads.
- default: `BOOTSTRAP=0` remains the ONE way to get a deliberately unoriented child, and it is
  an explicit human act. The refusal has an opt-OUT; it does not have a silent fallback.
- rejected-alternative: (defensible, and rejected) SPAWN anyway and hand the child
  `build_unmanifested_prompt`'s degraded prompt plus a banner — an instance that can SAY "I
  could not orient" is arguably better than no instance, and that is the shape shipped on
  2026-08-11. Rejected because it is exactly what was invisible: a booted-but-unoriented child
  still takes a channel, still takes the launcher singleton, still renders as a working session,
  and its one diagnostic goes to a discarding sink. Refusing costs one keypress to retry;
  degrading costs a session that looks right and is not.
- scenario:
  - when: a child is spawned under an id with no `<id>.meta.json` but whose role HAS a manifest
  - then: leg 3 resolves, the child is oriented from the role manifest, and the plate never appears
  - when: leg 3 resolves a ROLE manifest whose `id` DIFFERS from the instance id the child was spawned under
  - then: the child is oriented from the role's artifacts and is still named by its INSTANCE id — the role name never becomes the peer identity (TERM-C19)
  - when: neither `<id>.meta.json` nor `<role>.meta.json` exists and `BOOTSTRAP` is not `0`
  - then: no `pty.fork` occurs, the refusal plate names both paths in order, the refusal is published, and the widget is re-launchable
  - when: the same condition holds and the operator has set `BOOTSTRAP=0`
  - then: the child spawns unoriented, deliberately, and the status strip says `unoriented (BOOTSTRAP=0)`
- verify-machine: a chain test over all four legs with a fixture meta dir (explicit override,
  id-hit, role-hit, exhausted); a test asserting the exhausted case forks ZERO times and
  publishes the refusal payload; a test asserting `BOOTSTRAP=0` spawns and labels itself
  unoriented; a grep guard that NO host answers a missing manifest by logging and continuing.
- verify-machine: the role-hit leg's fixture manifest MUST carry an `id` that DIFFERS from the instance id under test, so leg 3 is exercised against a REAL role manifest rather than against the coincidence that made this invisible; a test asserting the resolved role manifest path is `intelligence/meta/<basename-of-role-descriptor>.meta.json` for a fixture descriptor; a test asserting a leg-3 manifest carrying `window_geometry` or `console`, or a literal `intelligence/<some-id>/in` under `channel.topics`, is REPORTED (these are instance-only keys, per `intelligence/meta/meta-object.schema.md`); and TERM-C19's exact-string assertion that the prompt names the INSTANCE.
- verify-human: David spawns a child under a role with no per-instance manifest and confirms it
  comes up ORIENTED off the role manifest; then removes the role manifest and confirms he gets a
  refusal he cannot miss rather than a session that merely feels vague.
- change: "2026-08-11 (fourth pass): leg 3's role name is now DERIVED — a role's name is the BASENAME OF ITS ROLE DESCRIPTOR (`.claude/agents/composeux-architect.md` -> `composeux-architect` -> `intelligence/meta/composeux-architect.meta.json`) — and the ROLE manifest is recorded as a DISTINCT ARTIFACT KIND from the instance manifest, with instance-only keys named and excluded. Previously this node said only `<role>.meta.json` and left both the role's NAME and the role manifest's CONTENT to convention."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "Leg 3 read `intelligence/meta/<role>.meta.json` with the role supplied by the caller as an unconstrained scalar (`TerminalWidget(role=...)`, defaulting to the bare `architect`), and no node said where a role NAME comes from or how a ROLE manifest differs from an INSTANCE manifest. Only one manifest had ever existed — `intelligence/meta/architect.meta.json`, an INSTANCE manifest — so leg 3 had never once resolved, and there was no artifact against which the distinction could be seen."
    rejected_alternative: "Keep a short curated role name with a registry mapping role -> descriptor -> manifest — rejected: a registry is a third place to keep in sync and the drift it permits is exactly what produced tonight's defects. Also rejected: derive the role manifest by COPYING the instance manifest wholesale — it was derived key-for-key with every `why` carried, but `window_geometry` and `console` are DROPPED and `channel.topics` is neutralised to a shape, because a wholesale copy is what makes a role file secretly instance-shaped."
    invalidating_assumption: "That one role descriptor file names exactly one role, and that its basename is unique across the fleet. If roles ever come to be composed from several descriptors, or if two agent files in different directories share a basename, the basename stops being an identity and leg 3 needs a qualified path (or the descriptor's own declared role id) instead — at which point this rule and `intelligence/meta/meta-object.schema.md` are re-read together, never one of them alone."
- upholds: [SBR-TRUST]
- relates-to: [CXC-ICL-COLD], [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 2. V1 scope — narrow by an explicit call, CHOSEN from measurement, and ENFORCED

### [CXC-TERM-SCOPE] V1 targets ONE mode, picked from the census among THREE candidates — `tui: fullscreen` is out either way

- decision: **(David's explicit call on the SHAPE)** V1 is deliberately narrower than the mode David
  runs
  today. `~/.claude/settings.json` currently carries `"tui": "fullscreen"` (verified 2026-08-10, line
  200), and fullscreen is OUT OF SCOPE for V1 whichever candidate wins.
- decision: **(the target itself is OPEN and is decided by MEASUREMENT, not by this document)**
  three
  candidates exist. The builder measures all of them ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS]) and V1's target is chosen from
  the evidence, with the choice and its reasoning recorded in [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE].

| Candidate | How it is selected | Status for V1 |
|-----------|--------------------|---------------|
| `tui: fullscreen` | `~/.claude/settings.json` (David's current setting) | REJECTED for V1 — the heaviest redraw profile; V2 |
| `tui: default` | `~/.claude/settings.json`, so it needs a per-launch settings override ([CXC-TERM-PIN]) | CANDIDATE |
| `--ax-screen-reader` | a per-invocation CLI FLAG — no shared state at all ([CXC-TERM-AXMODE]) | CANDIDATE, and structurally the stronger one |
| `--ax-screen-reader` + `tui: default` | both | CANDIDATE — the two are NOT mutually exclusive and the combination MUST be measured |
- requirement: `tui: fullscreen` MUST be handled by the explicit VISIBLE degradation path
  ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]), never by a best-effort render. A half-working fullscreen render is the failure
  mode this scope boundary exists to prevent.
- rationale: **(why any of these survived and `-p` did not)** unlike `-p`, neither the `tui` setting
  nor
  `--ax-screen-reader` restricts slash commands. `/compact` self-direction is fully preserved in ALL
  THREE. That single asymmetry is the reason a narrow-mode V1 is a legitimate host and a headless V1 is
  not.
- migration-path-to-v2: **(stated now so it is a widening, not a rewrite)** fullscreen support is a
  strictly
  ADDITIVE set of capabilities on the same emulator — alt-screen buffer switching (`?1049h`/`?1049l`),
  absolute cursor addressing, scroll regions (DECSTBM), and `2J`. `pyte.Screen` already implements all
  four ([CXC-TERM-EMULATOR]); V2's work is the RENDERER dropping its scrolling line model for a fixed
  screen-buffer model, plus a second census. Nothing in V1's seams is invalidated by that change —
  which is the test of whether this boundary was drawn in the right place.
- constraint: if the census shows that EVERY candidate emits alt-screen or absolute cursor addressing at
  steady state, this scope boundary buys nothing, V1 needs a screen buffer, and the estimate changes
  materially. The build STOPS and re-ratifies rather than proceeding on a false premise
  ([CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS]).
- scenario:
  - when: the child emits `\x1b[?1049h` (alt-screen entry) at any point
  - then: the widget enters the DEGRADED state with a visible banner naming the sequence
    ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]) — it does NOT attempt a fullscreen render
- verify-machine: a test feeds `\x1b[?1049h` into the widget's byte path and asserts the degraded state
  and the banner text; a second test asserts the widget never silently consumes it.
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-AXMODE], [CXC-TERM-PIN]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-AXMODE] `--ax-screen-reader` — a CLI flag, not a setting, and that difference is the structural argument

- premise: David, 2026-08-10 — "Another option to consider is launching the claude instance in screen
  reader mode (`claude --ax-screen-reader`) while dialing down the hold time value in
  `CLAUDE_AX_STARTUP_QUIET_MS` to 0 to prevent delay afforded for the screen reader to speak your
  prompts."
- definition: `claude --help` documents `--ax-screen-reader` as "Render screen-reader friendly output
  (flat text, no decorative borders or animations)."
- rationale: **(three reasons it may beat `tui: default` as V1's target)**

| # | Property | Consequence for this widget |
|---|----------|-----------------------------|
| 1 | It is a PER-INVOCATION FLAG, not a persisted setting | The widget-hosted instance uses it while every ordinary terminal session David runs keeps `fullscreen`. No shared state, no settings-file composition ([CXC-TERM-PIN] disappears entirely), and no risk of one launch path clobbering the other's mode |
| 2 | Its output is DESIGNED to be consumed as plain text | "Flat text, no decorative borders or animations" is close to a direct statement that it avoids the expensive parts — which is exactly the property a minimal renderer wants |
| 3 | Slash commands are unrestricted | `/compact` self-direction survives, so it clears the bar that disqualified `-p` ([CXC-TERM-REJECT-HEADLESS]) |
- requirement: if `--ax-screen-reader` is chosen, the widget MUST set `CLAUDE_AX_STARTUP_QUIET_MS=0` in
  the child's environment. That hold exists to let a speech synthesiser catch up on startup prompts; for
  a widget it is pure latency with no benefit.
- default: `AX_STARTUP_QUIET_MS` = `0` whenever the flag is used. Stated, not inferred.
- known-hazard: **(do not assume "flat text" means "no escape sequences")** screen-reader-friendly
  output is
  still a TUI. It may retain colour, cursor movement, and line editing — the flag's promise is about
  DECORATION, not about the control plane. The census measures it like every other candidate; the
  help-text wording is a hypothesis, not a finding.
- constraint: **(unmeasured interaction, flagged rather than assumed)** whether `--ax-screen-reader`
  interacts with the `tui` setting at all — overrides it, composes with it, or is orthogonal — is NOT
  known. The census MUST measure the combination as its own profile rather than reasoning about it.
- scenario:
  - when: the widget launches with `--ax-screen-reader`
  - then: no settings file is composed or written, `CLAUDE_AX_STARTUP_QUIET_MS=0` is in the child env,
    and the user's global `tui: fullscreen` is untouched
- verify-machine: a test asserts the argv carries `--ax-screen-reader`, that the env carries
  `CLAUDE_AX_STARTUP_QUIET_MS=0`, and that NO settings file is written on this path.
- relates-to: [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-PIN] IF the target is a `tui` mode, the boundary is UNENFORCEABLE without a per-launch pin — and `/tui` cannot be switched mid-session

- known-hazard: **(LOAD-BEARING; found while reading the actual config)** the `tui` setting is read
  from the
  user's `~/.claude/settings.json`. David's currently says `"tui": "fullscreen"`. A widget that spawns
  `claude` with no explicit settings therefore gets FULLSCREEN — the exact mode V1 does not support —
  and [CXC-TERM-SCOPE] becomes a comment rather than a constraint.
- requirement: **(CONDITIONAL on [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE] choosing a `tui` mode)** the widget's spawn
  MUST pin
  the mode explicitly by passing a settings file via `--settings <path>`, and MUST NOT rely on the
  user's global settings for anything V1's correctness depends on. If `--ax-screen-reader` is chosen
  instead, this entire requirement DISAPPEARS — which is [CXC-TERM-AXMODE]'s reason 1, and is a real
  reduction in surface area, not a stylistic preference.
- requirement: **(the pinned file is COMPOSED, not a fork)** the pinned settings file MUST be
  derived from
  the user's own settings with `tui` overridden — NOT a hand-written minimal file. A minimal file would
  silently drop the user's model choice, permissions, and MCP config, producing an instance that is
  subtly not the one David configured. It is written to the instance home ([CXC-ICL-CWD]) at launch.
- constraint: **(the mode is a LAUNCH-TIME decision, not a runtime one — stated because it WILL
  confuse someone)** `/tui` cannot be switched while background tasks are running — Claude Code
  refuses with
  "Cannot switch renderers while work is running in the background". A widget that hosts a session which
  itself dispatches background agents (which this one does, routinely) may simply have a mode switch
  REFUSED mid-session. Therefore: NO design in this spec may assume the mode can be changed at runtime.
  The mode is fixed at spawn, and changing it means a new session.
- default: `TARGET_MODE` = the value [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE] records once the census lands. An operator may
  override it per launch; a value V1 does not support is accepted, applied, and immediately produces the
  DEGRADED banner — the honest behaviour, not a refusal to launch.
- scenario:
  - when: a `tui`-mode target is chosen and the widget launches while `~/.claude/settings.json` carries
    `"tui": "fullscreen"`
  - then: the child runs under the PINNED mode, and the pinned file still carries the user's
    model/permissions/MCP settings
- scenario:
  - when: something attempts to switch the renderer mid-session while a background task runs
  - then: the switch is refused by Claude Code and the widget does NOT retry, re-pin, or restart the
    child on its own
- verify-machine: a test asserts the composed settings file sets the target mode AND preserves at least
  one non-`tui` key from a fixture user-settings file; a grep guard asserts no code path attempts a
  runtime `/tui` switch.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 3. The FIRST build task is a MEASUREMENT — do not inherit the architect's numbers

### [CXC-TERM-CENSUS] Produce a steady-state escape-sequence census for ALL FOUR profiles, and DERIVE V1's target AND supported set from it

- requirement: (this is build task #1, and it GATES everything) the builder MUST produce an
  escape-sequence census of a real `claude` session for all four candidate profiles —
  `tui: fullscreen`, `tui: default`, `--ax-screen-reader`, and `--ax-screen-reader` + `tui: default`
  ([CXC-TERM-SCOPE]). V1's TARGET MODE ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]) and its supported-sequence set
  ([CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE]) are both DERIVED from that measurement.
- requirement: (the three validity conditions — a capture missing ANY of them is not a census)

| # | Condition | Why, and how a run fails it |
|---|-----------|------------------------------|
| 1 | The session has actually CLEARED ONBOARDING/TRUST | Both prior attempts almost certainly did not ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR]). A child sitting on a trust gate emits a gate, not a TUI. The tool MUST assert it reached a usable prompt — a recognizable prompt marker plus a non-trivial byte count — and ABORT if it did not |
| 2 | **STEADY STATE**, not startup | Capture begins at the first response byte after a SUBMITTED prompt. This is where a TUI does its heaviest redrawing; startup is a different, lighter profile and mixing the two makes a census unreadable. Startup is captured and reported SEPARATELY |
| 3 | A RESIZE event is included | Reflow is the other expensive behaviour and the one most likely to reveal absolute addressing or scroll regions. The tool issues a `TIOCSWINSZ` + `SIGWINCH` mid-capture and reports the resulting burst as its own segment |
- requirement: (a FOURTH validity condition, added 2026-08-11 — the AS-DEPLOYED profile) the
  census MUST additionally run one profile under the configuration the WIDGET ACTUALLY LAUNCHES
  — the real `.mcp.json` (channel server included), `--dangerously-load-development-channels`,
  and permission prompts NOT suppressed — so the STARTUP segment contains the dialogs the human
  will actually answer. The five profiles measured on 2026-08-10 all ran
  `--strict-mcp-config --mcp-config '{"mcpServers": {}}'` with
  `skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt: true`, and NONE of them had a dialog
  ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]). The existing profiles are NOT retired — they remain the
  clean-room comparison that makes the sequence sets discriminable; the as-deployed profile is
  what makes any claim about STARTUP admissible.
- requirement: (the census must DISCRIMINATE) the census is valid only if the profiles differ
  MEASURABLY from each other. Byte-identical or class-identical output across modes is a PROBE FAILURE,
  not a finding, and the tool MUST FAIL LOUDLY rather than print a table.
- requirement: (what is recorded, per profile) total bytes; the count of each sequence class; the full
  set of DISTINCT CSI/OSC/DCS sequences observed with counts; the maximum burst size and inter-burst gap
  (the inputs to the coalescing cadence in [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE]); and the resize segment separately.
- default: (the measurement window) `CENSUS_STEADY_S` = **20.0 s** from the first response byte. The
  prompt submitted is a fixed fixture prompt that reliably produces a multi-second streaming response,
  committed with the tool so the measurement is repeatable.
- constraint: the census tool is COMMITTED (under `intelligence/tools/` or `python/tests/`), not a
  scratch script — [CXC-TERM-DEGRADE] re-runs its sequence table at runtime and V2 re-runs the whole
  tool. Two throwaway probes have already been written and discarded; that is how this measurement gets
  made a third time.
- confidence:
    value: 0.35
    blast_radius: high
    rationale: "V1's target mode, its supported-sequence set, and therefore whether V1 is a small line renderer or a full screen-buffer emulator all rest on a measurement that has FAILED TWICE ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR]). Everything downstream of [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS] is provisional. The value is below 0.5 because the failure mode of both prior attempts (never reaching a usable prompt) is a harness problem that has not yet been solved, not merely a run that was not done."
    assessed_by: "composeux-architect"
    assessed_at: "2026-08-10"
- scenario:
  - when: the census runs across the four profiles
  - then: all three validity conditions are asserted, the profiles DIFFER in at least one sequence
    class, and the report names which — and names the recommended V1 target with its reasoning
- scenario:
  - when: the child never reaches a usable prompt (a trust/onboarding gate)
  - then: the tool ABORTS with that diagnosis and produces NO profile — a zero-byte or gate-only capture
    is never reported as a finding
- scenario:
  - when: two profiles are byte-identical or class-identical
  - then: the run is declared a PROBE FAILURE, the defect is diagnosed ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR]), and no
    supported-set and no target choice are derived from it
- verify-machine: the tool's own selftest asserts it ABORTS on a fixture capture that never reached a
  prompt, and ABORTS on two identical fixture profiles.
- verify-human: David reads the census report and confirms the chosen profile matches what he sees on
  his glass.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY] A census that EXCLUDES the configuration actually run measures a system nobody uses

- definition: (the general rule this instance proves) a measurement is admissible as evidence
  about a system only over the CONFIGURATION that system is actually run in. Excluding the
  awkward parts of the real configuration does not produce a cleaner measurement of the same
  system; it produces a clean measurement of a DIFFERENT system — one nobody runs — and every
  timing or capability claim derived from it is unfounded.
- evidence: (the instance, measured 2026-08-11) all five profiles in
  `intelligence/tools/terminal_census_report/census.md` ran with
  `--strict-mcp-config --mcp-config '{"mcpServers": {}}'` (an EMPTY MCP config), with
  `--setting-sources local` and `skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt: true`, and WITHOUT
  `--dangerously-load-development-channels`. The report's validity table records `gates
  answered: none` for every one of them. Those exclusions were deliberate and individually
  well-reasoned — the tool's own docstring gives the safety rationale, and it is a good one:
  a census must not join ComposeBus, run the SessionStart hooks, or touch the live Architect's
  `self/*`. The defect is not the safety; it is that a STARTUP claim was then read off a
  startup that had been engineered to have no dialogs in it.
- consequence: (what it cost, concretely) `ptycore.py:222` states that the census measured
  `bracketed_paste` in the STARTUP segment of all five profiles, and concludes "so a `claude`
  child has `?2004h` on by the time anything is typed into it". On the first live spawn — real
  MCP config, dev channels flag, permission prompts live — the bootstrap fired into a blocking
  `Enter y/n:` read and `^[[200~` ECHOED AS LITERAL TEXT, because the TUI had not started and
  nothing had set `?2004h`. The claim was true of the measured configuration and false of the
  deployed one.
- constraint: this is the same shape as a control fitted to what already works. A gate that
  cannot fail, and a measurement whose configuration has had the failure modes removed, are one
  error wearing two costumes — both produce a green signal that carries no information.
- requirement: no node in this spec may cite a census figure about STARTUP that was not measured
  under the as-deployed profile ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS]'s fourth validity condition). Steady-state
  and resize figures from the clean-room profiles remain admissible for what they measure —
  redraw cost and sequence classes — because those do not depend on the excluded surfaces.
- scenario:
  - when: the census runs and the as-deployed profile records zero dialogs
  - then: that is itself reported as a FINDING to be explained (the human's prior grants make it
    plausible), never assumed to generalize — the profile is re-run on a clean grant state
- verify-machine: a test asserts the as-deployed profile's argv carries the real `.mcp.json` and
  does NOT carry `--strict-mcp-config`, an empty `mcpServers`, or
  `skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt`; a grep guard asserts no code comment cites a startup
  census figure without naming the profile it came from.
- verify-human: David reads the as-deployed profile's startup capture and recognizes the dialog
  sequence he actually answers.
- mitigates: [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS]
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR], [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR] TWO prior census attempts, BOTH VOID — discount them entirely, and the defects are named

- definition: two attempts were made (scratch probes, 2026-08-10, architect). Both are DISCOUNTED by
  their author. Neither produced a usable number.

| Attempt | Method | Result | Verdict |
|---------|--------|--------|---------|
| 1 | 6 s of STARTUP under `pty.fork()`, `tui: fullscreen` vs `tui: default` | **byte-identical output — 1436 bytes each** | VOID — failed to discriminate. Identical output for two modes that visibly differ on the glass is a probe failure, not a finding |
| 2 | settle, then type characters to force redraws without an API call | `fullscreen` captured **ZERO bytes** after typing; `--ax-screen-reader` captured 125 bytes / 23 escape sequences / 2 relative cursor moves | VOID — a zero-byte capture means the harness never reached a usable prompt, so the comparison is meaningless. The 125-byte figure is not a steady-state profile either |
- finding: **(the author's own downgrade, recorded so nobody promotes it later)** the hypothesis in
  [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS] — no alt-screen, no absolute cursor addressing, no scroll regions, no clears —
  is **UNCONFIRMED**. It may still be true; it has NOT been shown. No node in this spec may be
  implemented as if it had been.
- known-hazard: **(diagnosis A, the LEADING one for attempt 2 and likely attempt 1)** the harness
  almost
  certainly never cleared a **trust/onboarding gate** — a gate `--bare` does not clear. A child sitting
  on that gate emits the gate and nothing else, identically, in every mode. This is validity condition 1
  in [CXC-TERM-CENSUS].
- known-hazard: **(diagnosis B, and it is ALSO a requirement for the widget itself)** **nothing in
  this
  repository ever sets the PTY window size.** A grep across `intelligence/` for `TIOCSWINSZ`, `winsize`,
  `SIGWINCH` and `ioctl` returns ZERO matches — not in `launcher.py`, not in either probe. A pty created
  by `pty.fork()` has an unset (0x0) window size, and a TUI that cannot learn its dimensions degrades to
  minimal line-oriented output — identically, in every mode. It explains attempt 1's result, and it is
  exactly the bug that would make the real widget mis-render too ([CXC-TERM-WINSIZE]).
- known-hazard: **(diagnosis C, cheapest to rule out)** the probes passed `--bare` alongside
  `--settings`.
  If that flag is not accepted, 1436 identical bytes may simply be an identical usage or error message.
- requirement: the new census tool MUST (a) assert the child cleared onboarding and reached a usable
  prompt, ABORTING otherwise; (b) set the PTY window size via `TIOCSWINSZ` before the child reads it;
  (c) measure at STEADY STATE with a submitted prompt and a streaming response, plus a resize segment;
  and (d) FAIL LOUDLY when profiles do not differ, rather than printing a table.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS] The demanding-set hypothesis — UNCONFIRMED, to be CONFIRMED or REFUTED, never assumed

- status: UNCONFIRMED. Its author has explicitly downgraded the evidence behind it to zero
  ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR]: both probe attempts VOID). It is recorded as a hypothesis to TEST, and as
  the reason V1 was scoped optimistically enough to be worth measuring — not as a finding.
- definition: the void runs SUGGESTED (and only suggested) that the demanding set for a narrow mode is:

| Class | Sequence family | Status |
|-------|-----------------|--------|
| mouse tracking | `?1000h` family | hypothesis: PRESENT |
| bracketed paste | `?2004h` | hypothesis: PRESENT |
| 256-colour SGR | `\x1b[…38;…m` | hypothesis: PRESENT |
| cursor hide | `?25l` | hypothesis: PRESENT |
| alt-screen | `?1049h` / `?47h` | hypothesis: ABSENT |
| absolute cursor addressing | `\x1b[r;cH` | hypothesis: ABSENT |
| scroll regions | DECSTBM `\x1b[t;br` | hypothesis: ABSENT |
| clear screen | `\x1b[2J` | hypothesis: ABSENT |
- consequence: **(IF CONFIRMED)** V1 is a **scrolling, line-oriented renderer** rather than a full
  screen-buffer emulator — a dramatically smaller build, and the justification for the whole V1 scope
  boundary.
- consequence: **(IF REFUTED (stated so the build has a defined branch, not a surprise))** the four
  "ABSENT"
  rows appearing at steady state in EVERY candidate means the narrow modes are screen-buffer TUIs, V1's
  renderer must be a screen-buffer renderer, [CXC-TERM-SCOPE]'s narrowing buys little, and **the
  estimate changes materially**. The build STOPS and re-ratifies rather than absorbing the change
  silently — and [DD-TERM-55-SEQUENCING] is re-read the same day, because 55's Terminal path stops being
  a short bridge.
- constraint: this table is a HYPOTHESIS with NO surviving evidence behind it. No node in this spec may
  be implemented as if it were measured, and no estimate may be given on its basis.
  [CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE] is where the measured truth lands.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE] ONE sequence table, two consumers — the census tool and the runtime degradation detector

- requirement: the supported-sequence set is a single committed data structure (a module-level table of
  `name -> compiled pattern -> supported: bool`), imported BY the census tool AND BY the widget's
  runtime detector. Two copies of that table is the fork-divergence class this corpus fails builds over
  ([DX-NARROW-WAIST]) — and the specific failure it produces is a widget that believes it supports a
  sequence the census says it does not.
- requirement: the widget PRE-SCANS each byte chunk against the table's UNSUPPORTED patterns BEFORE
  feeding the emulator, and counts every hit per sequence name. This is what makes degradation VISIBLE
  ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]) instead of silent: an emulator on its own simply drops what it does not
  implement, which is precisely the subtly-wrong render this spec forbids.
- requirement: **(chunk-boundary safety)** a sequence can be SPLIT across two PTY reads. The
  pre-scan MUST
  carry a tail buffer of `SEQ_TAIL_MAX` (**default 64** bytes) across chunks so a split sequence is
  matched once, not missed and not double-counted.
- default: the table is generated from the census and committed WITH the census report that produced it,
  so the provenance of every `supported: true` is one file away.
- scenario:
  - when: an unsupported sequence arrives split across two `os.read` chunks
  - then: the detector counts it exactly ONCE
- verify-machine: a test splits `\x1b[?1049h` at every byte offset and asserts exactly one count in each
  case; a test asserts the census tool and the widget import the SAME table object (identity, not a
  copy).
- uses: [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 4. Mechanism — reuse the PTY half, build only the render and input halves

### [CXC-TERM-PTYCORE] EXTRACT `launcher.py`'s PTY half to a shared core — do NOT fork it, and do NOT import `Launcher` whole

- definition: `launcher.py` already contains a ComposeApp-compatible PTY half. `_pump` is DELIBERATELY
  structured as a single select cycle over plain fds "so the selftest can drive it with pipes — no
  controlling terminal required". That structure is what makes this reuse possible at all.
- requirement: **(the split, stated precisely because a builder WILL get it wrong)** the following
  table is
  normative. The REUSE column is the extraction boundary.

| `launcher.py` member | Reusable in-process? | Disposition |
|----------------------|----------------------|-------------|
| `start_child()` (`pty.fork` + `execvp`) | YES | move to the shared core |
| `_pump(out_fd, timeout)` | YES, with one change | move to the core; the widget needs a **byte-sink callback**, not an `out_fd` — see below |
| `_write_all(fd, data)`, `_write_lock` | YES | move to the core |
| `inject(keys, kind)` / `inject_line(line, kind)` + the three-phase clear/type/submit | YES | move to the core — the widget needs the IDENTICAL actuation |
| `_child_exited()`, `shutdown()` | YES | move to the core |
| `_export_session_pin()`, `_derive_jsonl_path`, `project_dir_key` | YES | move to the core (the console data layer depends on the pin — [CXC-TERM-FACTS] fact 1) |
| `build_claude_args`, `CHANNEL_FLAGS` | YES | move to the core |
| `proxy_loop(in_fd, out_fd)` | **NO** | a blocking `while` loop; the widget is driven by the engine's frame loop |
| `_enter_raw(fd)` / termios / `tty.setraw` | **NO** | there is no controlling terminal in a windowed app |
| `_install_signals()` | **NO** | the console owns SIGTERM/SIGINT |
| `run()` | **NO** | it takes the singleton, enters raw mode, and blocks — it is a `main()`, not a component |
- requirement: **(`_pump`'s one change)** the core's pump takes an `on_output: Callable[[bytes],
  None]`
  sink instead of an `out_fd`. `launcher.py` passes a sink that does `_write_all(out_fd, data)` — so its
  behaviour is byte-identical — and the widget passes a sink that enqueues for the emulator. This is the
  ONLY behavioural change to the existing path, and it is required so the widget does not have to invent
  a pipe just to read its own child.
- constraint: **(zero behaviour change to the existing launcher)** after the extraction,
  `python3 intelligence/launcher/launcher.py --selftest` MUST pass unchanged, and the live
  `transport.sh` path MUST behave identically. The extraction is a MOVE, not a rewrite; any semantic
  change rides a separate commit.
- known-hazard: the tempting shortcut is `from launcher import Launcher` inside the widget. It imports a
  module that installs signal handlers and takes a superseding flock in `run()`, and whose module import
  path assumes `intelligence/launcher/` is on `sys.path`. Importing it whole couples a render widget to
  a CLI program's lifecycle. The extraction exists to prevent exactly that.
- scenario:
  - when: the extraction lands and `python3 intelligence/launcher/launcher.py --selftest` runs
  - then: it passes unchanged, and the live `transport.sh` path behaves identically — the move changed
    no behaviour
- scenario:
  - when: the widget and `launcher.py` each host a child
  - then: both drive the SAME pump and the SAME write path; `pty.fork(` appears exactly once in the tree
- verify-machine: the launcher selftest green post-extraction; a grep guard asserting `pty.fork(` and
  the select-pump appear EXACTLY ONCE in the tree; a test drives the core's pump with a pipe pair and a
  recording sink.
- uses: [DX-NARROW-WAIST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-WINSIZE] Set the PTY window size — the widget's geometry IS the terminal's geometry

- known-hazard: **(the same omission that probably voided the census)** nothing in the repo sets a
  PTY
  window size ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR]). A widget that inherits that omission gets a child rendering to
  an unknown-size terminal, wrapping at the wrong column, and — per the leading diagnosis — possibly
  refusing the requested tui mode entirely.
- requirement: the core MUST set the pty's window size via `ioctl(master_fd, TIOCSWINSZ, ...)` with the
  widget's computed `(rows, cols)` **BEFORE** the child's first read, i.e. between `pty.fork()`'s parent
  return and any output being consumed.
- requirement: **(resize is a live event, not a launch-time constant)** when the widget's arranged
  rect
  changes, it recomputes `(rows, cols)` ([CXC-TERM-GRID]), re-issues `TIOCSWINSZ`, and sends `SIGWINCH`
  to the child's process GROUP. It MUST also resize the emulator's screen in the same operation, so the
  emulator's dimensions and the child's belief about them can never disagree.
- requirement: **(debounce)** a drag-resize produces a resize per frame. The widget coalesces and
  issues at
  most one `TIOCSWINSZ` + `SIGWINCH` per `RESIZE_DEBOUNCE_S` (**default 0.15 s**), plus one final issue
  after motion stops. An un-debounced resize storm makes the child redraw continuously and is
  indistinguishable from a hang.
- default: pixel dimensions are also sent (`ws_xpixel`/`ws_ypixel` = the arranged rect's device pixels);
  zero is permitted and is what a program falls back from, but sending the real values costs nothing.
- scenario:
  - when: the widget is 80 columns wide and the human drags the window to 100 columns
  - then: within one debounce interval the child receives `TIOCSWINSZ` for 100 columns and a `SIGWINCH`,
    and re-wraps
- scenario:
  - when: the child is spawned
  - then: the window size is already set when the child first reads it — never 0x0
- verify-machine: a test asserts `TIOCSWINSZ` is issued before the first `on_output` callback; a resize
  test asserts exactly ONE ioctl+signal pair per debounce window under an injected clock; a test asserts
  the emulator's `(rows, cols)` equals the ioctl's after every resize.
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-GRID]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON] The console becomes A LAUNCHER — it MUST take the same superseding singleton, or a stray `transport.sh` reaps it

- known-hazard: **(LOAD-BEARING, and absent from the design brief)** `launcher.py` takes a
  **SUPERSEDING**
  singleton per `<id>` (`intelligence-launcher-<id>`) — a second launcher REAPS the first, because two
  launchers relaying `intelligence/<id>/out` would DOUBLE-INJECT every actuation ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON],
  [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD]). A console hosting the PTY and relaying `/compact` IS a launcher by that
  definition. If it does not participate in the lock, a human running `transport.sh architect` in a
  terminal will silently supersede — and then reap — the console's live child.
- requirement: the widget MUST acquire `intelligence-launcher-<id>` through the EXISTING
  `acquire_singleton_superseding` helper before spawning, and MUST hold it for the child's lifetime.
  The same helper, the same lock name, no second mechanism.
- requirement: **(the console must SURVIVE being superseded)** unlike a CLI launcher, the console
  cannot
  exit when it loses the lock. When a successor supersedes it, the widget MUST detect the loss, stop
  relaying, tear down its child cleanly, and render the DEGRADED banner "superseded by another launcher
  for <id>". A console that keeps a half-dead child while another launcher relays is the double-injection
  failure this lock exists to prevent.
- constraint: this spec MUST NOT change `acquire_singleton_superseding`'s semantics or invent a
  console-specific variant. If superseding turns out to be wrong for a windowed host, that is a
  re-ratification of [CXC-IC-SINGLETON], not a local exception.
- scenario:
  - when: the widget hosts a live child and a human runs `transport.sh architect` in a terminal
  - then: exactly ONE launcher relays; the loser tears down and says so on the glass — no silent
    double-injection, no orphaned child
- verify-machine: a test acquires the lock out-of-process, drives the widget's spawn, and asserts the
  widget either supersedes cleanly or degrades with the banner; a test asserts exactly one relay
  subscription exists per id.
- upholds: [CXC-IC-SINGLETON]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-EMULATOR] Use `pyte` for the screen/escape state machine — a NEW declared dependency

- decision: PROPOSED (recommended: YES) — `pyte` (pure Python, pip-installable, long-established) owns
  the escape-sequence state machine and the screen buffer. See [DD-TERM-PYTE].
- requirement: **(it is NOT currently installed — this is a real dependency decision)** `pyte` MUST
  be added
  to `requirements.txt` with a pin, in the same commit as the first code that imports it, with a scope
  comment in that file's established idiom. `requirements.txt` was created 2026-08-08 precisely so a new
  dependency has a file to be declared in; using it is the point.
- requirement: **(pin to a version verified on THIS interpreter)** the repo's observed-good set is
  macOS +
  Python 3.13. The pin MUST be a version the builder has imported and driven under 3.13, not the latest
  release number.
- requirement: **(the emulator is a LIBRARY, not the widget's brain)** the widget owns byte intake,
  damage,
  input translation, degradation, and lifecycle. `pyte` owns exactly one thing: bytes in, a character
  grid out. The widget MUST NOT subclass `pyte.Stream` to add behaviour; unsupported-sequence detection
  lives in [CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE]'s pre-scan, upstream of pyte, because pyte's own answer to an
  unimplemented sequence is to DROP IT SILENTLY — the exact behaviour this spec forbids.
- requirement: **(scrollback)** the widget uses `pyte.HistoryScreen`, not `pyte.Screen`. Under the
  line-oriented hypothesis the conversation scrolls off the top, and a terminal that cannot scroll back
  is not a usable host. Default history = `SCROLLBACK_LINES` **2000**.
- default: **(feed encoding)** bytes are decoded UTF-8 with `errors="replace"` through an
  INCREMENTAL
  decoder held across chunks, so a multi-byte character split across two `os.read` calls is not
  corrupted. A fresh `bytes.decode` per chunk is the bug this default forecloses.
- scenario:
  - when: a multi-byte UTF-8 character is split across two PTY reads
  - then: it renders as one correct glyph, not two replacement characters
- verify-machine: a test feeds a split emoji/CJK sequence and asserts one glyph; a test asserts
  `requirements.txt` names pyte and that the import succeeds under the repo interpreter.
- relates-to: [DD-TERM-PYTE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-GRID] The renderer — a monospace character grid on the existing text-drawing contract

- definition: the widget draws a `rows x cols` character grid using the Canvas text primitives the
  engine already has. Nearest prior art: `bus_monitor.py` (a bottom-anchored scrolling line log,
  `_LINE_H = 15.0`), `chat_thread.py`, `multiline_text.py`. No new Canvas primitive is required — if the
  builder finds they need one, that is a signal the design drifted; stop and re-ratify.
- requirement: **(metrics are MEASURED once, not assumed)** `CELL_W` is `canvas.measure_text("M")`
  at the
  chosen font/size (cached per font+size, invalidated on theme change), and `CELL_H` is the line height.
  Hardcoding a cell width makes the grid drift the moment the bundled font changes.
- defaults: **(every value stated, so nothing is guessed)**
  - `font` = `console_theme.FONT_MONO` (the bundled IBM Plex Mono — the same font every other console
    surface uses)
  - `TERM_FONT_PX` = `12.0`
  - `CELL_H` = `15.0` (matching `bus_monitor._LINE_H`, so the two log-shaped surfaces align)
  - `PAD` = `8.0` (logical px, all four sides)
  - `cols` = `floor((rect.w - 2*PAD) / CELL_W)`; `rows` = `floor((rect.h - 2*PAD) / CELL_H)`
  - `MIN_COLS` = `40`; `MIN_ROWS` = `10` — below either, the widget draws a "TERMINAL TOO SMALL —
    <cols>x<rows>, needs 40x10" plate and spawns NOTHING (it does not spawn a child it cannot render)
  - `SCROLLBACK_LINES` = `2000`
  - `CURSOR_BLINK_HZ` = `1.0`, matching `text_input`'s caret; `reduced_motion` renders a steady block
    ([CXC-ICV-CHROME] idiom)
  - colours: the 16 basic ANSI colours map through the console theme token table so the terminal reads
    as part of the console rather than a pasted-in xterm; colours 16-255 use the standard xterm cube +
    greyscale ramp. Default background = the theme's panel ground, NOT black
- requirement: **(the cursor is rendered from the emulator, not tracked separately)** position comes
  from
  `screen.cursor.x/y` and visibility from `screen.cursor.hidden`, so `?25l` is honoured for free. A
  second cursor model is a second source of truth.
- requirement: **(bottom-stick)** the view auto-follows the newest line while the scroll offset is
  zero, and
  STOPS following the moment the human scrolls up — resuming only when they return to the bottom.
  Auto-scrolling a human who is reading is the same discourtesy as a stale gauge.
- scenario:
  - when: the arranged rect is 300 px wide
  - then: the widget draws the TOO SMALL plate and no child is spawned
- scenario:
  - when: the human scrolls up 40 lines while output streams
  - then: the view stays put; new output accumulates below; returning to the bottom resumes following
- verify-machine: a golden capture of a fixture byte-stream at a fixed rect; a metrics test asserting
  `cols` is derived from `measure_text` and not a constant; a scroll test asserting the stick/unstick
  transitions.
- verify-human: David reads a real streaming response in the widget and confirms it is legible and
  correctly wrapped at the widget's edge.
- uses: [SPEC:CXC-RENDER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-INPUT] Keystrokes to the PTY — the engine gives NAMED keys + committed text, and Tab is currently EATEN

- definition: the engine delivers keyboard input on two channels (`window.py`): `SDL_KEYDOWN` becomes
  `on_key(name, modifiers)` where `name` is `SDL_GetKeyName` ("A", "Return", "Escape", "Left", "F1")
  and `modifiers` is the raw SDL KMOD bitmask; `SDL_TEXTINPUT` becomes `on_text(text)` with committed
  text. A terminal needs BYTES. The widget therefore owns a keymap: `(name, modifiers) -> bytes`.
- requirement: **(the double-delivery rule — this is how `text_input` already avoids it, stated
  explicitly because getting it wrong types every character twice)** printable characters are sent
  from `on_text`
  ONLY. `on_key` sends ONLY named/control keys and modified keys that produce no text event. A widget
  that sends from both channels double-types.
- requirement: (the minimum keymap, all of which V1 needs)

| Input | Bytes |
|-------|-------|
| printable text (`on_text`) | the UTF-8 encoding, verbatim |
| Return | `\r` (0x0D) — **not** `\n`; this is live-verified in `launcher.py`: the TUI inserts `\n` as a newline and submits on `\r` |
| Escape | `\x1b` |
| Backspace | `\x7f` |
| Tab / Shift-Tab | `\t` / `\x1b[Z` |
| Up/Down/Right/Left | `\x1b[A` / `\x1b[B` / `\x1b[C` / `\x1b[D` |
| Home/End/PageUp/PageDown | `\x1b[H` / `\x1b[F` / `\x1b[5~` / `\x1b[6~` |
| Delete | `\x1b[3~` |
| Ctrl-<letter> | the control byte (`Ctrl-C` -> `\x03`, `Ctrl-D` -> `\x04`, `Ctrl-L` -> `\x0c`, …) |
| Alt-<key> | `\x1b` prefix + the key's bytes |
- known-hazard: (an ENGINE change is required, and the brief does not mention it) `FocusManager.handle_key`
  (`python/composeio/render/engine/focus.py`, line 66) intercepts `Tab` and moves focus — the focused
  widget NEVER sees it. Claude Code's TUI uses Tab. Without a change, the terminal widget cannot receive
  the key at all.
- requirement: (the fix follows the EXISTING opt-out idiom, it does not invent one) the focus ring
  already has a per-widget opt-out (`focus_ring = False`, consulted with `getattr` so any widget lacking
  the attribute keeps the default). Tab capture uses the identical shape: a widget attribute
  `captures_tab` (**default False**), consulted with `getattr` in `handle_key` before the Tab branch. No
  existing widget's behaviour changes. See [DD-TERM-TAB-CAPTURE] for how the human then leaves the
  widget.
- requirement: (the escape hatch is MANDATORY) with `captures_tab` true and Escape forwarded to the
  child, a human can become unable to leave the widget by keyboard. The widget MUST expose an explicit
  release: `RELEASE_CHORD` (**default Ctrl-Shift-Escape**) blurs the widget and returns focus to the
  engine, is NOT forwarded to the child, and is rendered in the widget's status strip so it is
  discoverable rather than remembered.
- requirement: (⌘V — ONE branch on the EXISTING paste path, not a second paste implementation)
  `on_text` (`terminal.py:838-848`) already wraps a multi-character payload in bracketed-paste
  markers per [CXC-TERM-PASTE]. The clipboard never reaches it because `terminal.py:821` —
  `if (mod & _KMOD_GUI) and len(key) == 1: return`, "Cmd-shortcuts belong to the app, not the
  child" — swallows ⌘V before anything reads the clipboard. The fix is one branch ABOVE that
  return: on ⌘V the widget reads the SDL clipboard and routes the text through the EXISTING
  `on_text` path, so bracketed-paste wrapping, the length rule, and the bottom-stick follow all
  apply unchanged. A second write path for pasted text would fork the one thing
  [CXC-TERM-PASTE] exists to guarantee. Every other Cmd-chord keeps belonging to the app.
- default: an EMPTY or unavailable clipboard on ⌘V writes ZERO bytes — no marker pair, no
  keystroke. A bracketed-paste wrapper around nothing is a submit the human did not ask for.
- out: (⌘C — scoped OUT explicitly, and this is the reason) copy-out requires a SELECTION MODEL,
  and neither the widget nor the engine has one — `terminal.py` contains no selection state and
  the grid has no anchor/extent concept ([CXC-TERM-OUT] already scopes text selection out of
  V1). ⌘C with nothing to copy is not a smaller feature, it is the selection slice with its
  visible half removed. It lands WITH selection or not at all.
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses ⌘V with three lines on the clipboard and the child has `?2004h` set
  - then: the payload arrives through `on_text`, wrapped once, and is entered as ONE multi-line
    prompt — the identical bytes a middle-click paste would have produced
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses ⌘V with an empty clipboard
  - then: nothing is written to the child
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses any other Cmd-chord
  - then: it is swallowed for the app exactly as today, and the child sees nothing
- requirement: (write through the shared lock) every keystroke is written through the core's
  `_write_lock` ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]), because the actuation relay writes to the same fd from a bus
  thread. An unlocked write interleaves a keystroke into the middle of an injected `/compact` line.
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses `Ctrl-C` while a response streams
  - then: `\x03` reaches the child and the response is interrupted, exactly as in a real terminal
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses Tab while the widget is focused and `captures_tab` is true
  - then: `\t` reaches the child and focus does NOT move
- scenario:
  - when: the human presses the release chord
  - then: focus leaves the widget, nothing is written to the child, and the strip's hint matches
- verify-machine: a keymap table test over every row above; a test asserting no printable character is
  sent from BOTH `on_key` and `on_text`; a focus test asserting Tab reaches a `captures_tab` widget and
  still moves focus for every other widget; a test asserting the release chord writes zero bytes;
  a ⌘V test asserting the bytes are IDENTICAL to the same text delivered through `on_text`
  (proving one path, not two), an empty-clipboard test asserting zero bytes, and a test
  asserting every other Cmd-chord still writes nothing.
- verify-human: David copies a multi-line block from another app, presses ⌘V in the widget, and
  it arrives as one prompt rather than several submitted fragments.
- uses: [CXC-R-ENGINE-TEXT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-PASTE] Bracketed paste — honour `?2004h`, because the alternative is the known bootstrap failure

- requirement: the widget tracks the child's bracketed-paste mode (set by `?2004h`, cleared by
  `?2004l` — the emulator already parses it) and, when ENABLED, wraps multi-character paste payloads in
  `\x1b[200~` … `\x1b[201~`.
- rationale: **(why this small thing is in V1 at all)** a multi-line paste delivered raw SUBMITS ON
  EVERY
  EMBEDDED NEWLINE, because the TUI reads `\r`/`\n` as submit. That is the documented historical failure
  of the re-register bootstrap — named as the invalidating assumption of [DD-ICL-COLD-VS-RESUME]
  ("a multi-line prompt submitting early on an embedded newline is its known historical failure"). The
  widget would reproduce a known, already-paid-for defect. Five lines of wrapping prevents it.
- requirement: **(paste arrives as text, not keys)** a paste is delivered by SDL as text input, so
  it enters
  through `on_text`. The widget MUST distinguish a paste-sized payload from a keystroke by LENGTH
  (`> 1` character) rather than by a paste event, since the engine surfaces no distinct paste event
  today.
- default: when bracketed paste is NOT enabled by the child, a multi-line paste is sent verbatim — the
  same behaviour a real terminal has, and therefore not a regression.
- scenario:
  - when: the child has enabled `?2004h` and the human pastes three lines
  - then: the payload arrives wrapped and is entered as ONE multi-line prompt, not submitted three times
- verify-machine: a test enables `?2004h` via the byte path, drives a multi-line `on_text`, and asserts
  the exact wrapped bytes; a test with paste mode off asserts verbatim bytes.
- relates-to: [DD-ICL-COLD-VS-RESUME]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE] Control-is-a-peer, COMPLETED — a bus keystroke and a keyboard keystroke are the same act

- definition: the launcher's `inject` actuation already types arbitrary text into the child's PTY from a
  BUS message ("a general 'enter this on my behalf, as if the human typed it' actuation — the primitive
  the ComposeApp collaboration model builds on; `/compact` is just its structured special case").
  Hosting the PTY in a widget makes the keyboard the SECOND writer of that same fd.
- requirement: both paths MUST converge on the core's single write path ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]), under the
  single lock, with the SAME audit log. The widget MUST NOT open a second write path for "human" input.
- consequence: **(this is the claim, stated so it is testable rather than rhetorical)** the child
  cannot
  distinguish a keystroke published on the bus from a keystroke typed on the glass, because there is
  only one code path that reaches it. That is acceptance criterion TERM-C8, and it is satisfied by
  CONSTRUCTION — the test asserts the structure, not the behaviour.
- requirement: **(the audit surface survives)** `injection_log` records every launcher-originated
  write
  today. Human keystrokes MUST NOT be logged into it — the log's meaning is "writes the harness
  originated", and polluting it with keyboard traffic destroys the audit property AND leaks the human's
  typing into a durable record. The rule: bus-originated writes are logged; keyboard writes are not.
- scenario:
  - when: a peer publishes `{"cmd":"inject","text":"hello"}` on `intelligence/<id>/out`
  - then: the child receives the identical bytes it would have received had the human typed "hello" and
    pressed Return, through the identical three-phase clear/type/submit
- verify-machine: a test drives both paths against a recording fake child and asserts byte-identical
  output; a grep guard asserting exactly ONE `_write_all(self.master_fd` call site; a test asserting
  keyboard input does not append to `injection_log`.
- realizes: [CXC-LB-CREDO]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV] The cold-start bootstrap rides ARGV — and the startup INJECTION path is DELETED, not fixed

- premise: (measured, the FIRST LIVE SPAWN, 2026-08-11) the bootstrap fired into a blocking
  `Enter y/n:` read — the channel-approval dialog — during startup, and `^[[200~` echoed as
  LITERAL TEXT because the TUI had not started and nothing had set `?2004h`.
- constraint: (the root cause is the MECHANISM, not the number) `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY_S = 3.0` is a
  FIXED TIMER standing in for a STATE CHECK, against a startup that contains an UNBOUNDED number
  of human-gated blocking reads — how many depends on the flags, on which grants the human has
  already given, and on how fast they type. There is no value of that timer that is correct for
  anyone's startup, so tuning it is not a fix and neither is a bigger default.
- definition: (the capability that makes the whole path unnecessary) `claude` takes a POSITIONAL
  prompt. `claude --help` on this machine, 2026-08-11:
  `Usage: claude [options] [command] [prompt]` / `Arguments: prompt   Your prompt`.
- requirement: (David's ruling, settled — this DELETES rather than fixes) the cold-start
  re-register bootstrap is delivered as the child's POSITIONAL PROMPT ARGUMENT at exec time. The
  following are REMOVED from the tree, not deprecated: `ptycore.BOOTSTRAP_DELAY_S`,
  `ptycore.bootstrap_delay_s()` and its `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` env read, `Launcher.arm_bootstrap()`,
  `terminal.py::_arm_bootstrap` / `_type_bootstrap`, and the `threading.Timer` that armed them.
  The `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` knob disappears from the config tables, the launcher docstring and
  `intelligence/launcher/README.md` in the same commit — a retired knob left documented is the
  contradiction [CXC-ICL-COLD] already had to correct once.
- consequence: (this is why it is a deletion and not a refactor) NOTHING is EVER typed at
  startup. [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS] is then satisfied BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by careful timing —
  its verify-machine's carve-out ("the bootstrap timer excepted") is deleted with the timer, and
  "zero widget-originated bytes before the first human keypress" becomes unconditional. Under
  Principle II.13 this moves the bootstrap from tier 4 (PTY scraping / timing against a TUI) to
  tier 1 (structured harness input).
- requirement: (WHAT argv carries — the EXPLICIT form, David's call) the argument is the prompt
  built by the EXISTING `build_bootstrap_for` ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED] is unchanged and
  still governs its CONTENT), and its FIRST LINE is an explicit instruction naming the manifest
  by ABSOLUTE path — `Read <abs>/intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json and complete the bootstrap it
  describes`. Self-contained, no discovery step, and it still resolves for a child spawned
  outside this repo. The bare `complete bootstrap` form is REJECTED
  ([DD-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV-FORM]).
- amended: (2026-08-11, RATIFIED at build time — the DELIVERY is INDIRECT, and this bullet is
  normative over the one above where they differ) the generated prompt is NOT the argv element.
  The generator still runs, unchanged; its ~6 KB output is WRITTEN TO A FILE
  (`$TMPDIR/composeio-bootstrap/bootstrap-<id>-<ts>.md`, `BOOTSTRAP_PROMPT_DIR` overrides), and
  argv carries a SHORT instruction naming that file by ABSOLUTE path — `Read <abs-prompt-file>
  and complete the bootstrap it describes. Follow it exactly, including every artifact it
  names.` The EXPLICIT form is preserved exactly one level in: the FILE's own first line names
  the MANIFEST by absolute path, so a cold instance still has no discovery step.
- evidence: (why the indirect form, and it is the only one with live evidence) David typed this
  exact shape by hand on 2026-08-11 and the child read all NINE artifacts and ran
  `lts_selfcheck` — a step that exists only in the CITED spec, which is how we know the pointer
  was FOLLOWED rather than paraphrased. Inlining 6 KB of prose in an argv element has no such
  evidence, is the form the human never used, and puts the whole body through an exec boundary
  and a process listing for no gain.
- consequence: `BOOTSTRAP_ARGV_MAX` now bounds the INSTRUCTION rather than the body, so the
  truncation hazard it was written against cannot arise at all; the refusal remains, because a
  bound that can never be hit is still cheaper than a bound that is silently absent.
- constraint: (the deletion has a HARD boundary) it removes the STARTUP seam only. The
  mid-session actuation seam is a different mechanism against a different state and is
  explicitly out of its blast radius — [CXC-TERM-MIDSESSION-SEAM] states that boundary and is
  the guard the deletion is reviewed against.
- default: `BOOTSTRAP_ARGV_MAX` = **131072** bytes. A generated prompt above it is a REFUSAL
  naming the size, never a truncated exec — a silently clipped orientation prompt is the
  unoriented-instance failure with a longer fuse ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]).
- known-hazard: (honestly unmeasured, and it is the reason this has a glass gate) whether the
  harness submits a positional prompt BEFORE, AFTER, or INSTEAD OF its startup dialogs is not
  measured. What the ruling guarantees is narrower and is the part that matters: WE never type,
  so whatever the harness does with the prompt, no keystroke of ours can collide with a human
  answering a gate. The as-deployed census profile ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]) is where
  the ordering gets measured, and TERM-C13 is where David confirms it.
- scenario:
  - when: a cold session is spawned in the widget with dialogs pending
  - then: the child's argv carries the prompt, ZERO bytes are written to the master fd before
    the human's first keypress, and no `^[[200~` appears anywhere on the glass
- scenario:
  - when: the instance later emits `{"cmd":"compact","direction":"…"}` on its channel
  - then: the three-phase injection fires exactly as before — the mid-session seam is unaffected
- scenario:
  - when: the built prompt exceeds `BOOTSTRAP_ARGV_MAX`
  - then: the spawn is refused with the size named; no truncated prompt is ever exec'd
- verify-machine: a test asserts the child argv's last element IS the bootstrap prompt and that
  its first line names the absolute manifest path; a REAL-spawn test asserting ZERO bytes are
  written to the master fd for the whole session up to the first synthetic keypress
  (unconditional now — no timer carve-out); grep guards that `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY`,
  `arm_bootstrap` and `_type_bootstrap` appear NOWHERE in the tree, and that `threading.Timer`
  has no bootstrap call site; a test asserting `{"cmd":"compact"}` and `{"cmd":"inject"}` still
  produce byte-identical output to the pre-deletion path.
- verify-human: David cold-starts a session in the widget, answers whatever dialogs appear, and
  confirms the instance arrives ORIENTED with no stray paste markers and nothing typed under his
  hands. Mandatory, non-delegable — this is the failure he saw.
- change: "2026-08-11, FIRST LIVE SPAWN: bootstrap delivery moves from a timed PTY injection to the child's positional prompt argument, and the entire startup-injection path (`BOOTSTRAP_DELAY_S`, the settle mechanism, `arm_bootstrap`/`_type_bootstrap`) is DELETED. Mid-session `{\"cmd\":\"compact\"}` / `{\"cmd\":\"inject\"}` actuation is explicitly untouched."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "`ptycore.BOOTSTRAP_DELAY_S = 3.0` with a `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` env override; `Launcher.arm_bootstrap()` and `terminal.py::_arm_bootstrap` each armed a `threading.Timer(delay, _type_bootstrap)` that wrote the prompt into the PTY through the three-phase inject. [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS]'s verify-machine carried an explicit carve-out for it ('the bootstrap timer excepted'), and `ptycore.py:222` justified the bracketed-paste wrapping with a census figure claiming `?2004h` was already on."
    reasoning: "A fixed delay is a state check that cannot fail loudly: when it is wrong it does not error, it types into whatever happens to be reading. The startup it races contains an unbounded number of human-gated blocking reads, so the mechanism is unsound for every possible value, not mis-tuned. `claude` accepting a positional prompt means the whole race is avoidable rather than winnable — which is why the ruling deletes the path instead of hardening it."
    rejected_alternative: "Detect readiness by watching the child's output for a prompt marker — this is the BANNED shape: [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS] and [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY] forbid output pattern-matching at startup permanently, because an earlier launcher that did it collided with the human and HUNG. Also rejected: raise the delay, or make it configurable per launch — both keep a timer standing in for a state check and merely move the failure to a slower machine or a chattier grant state."
    counter_argument: "The bootstrap was working in the CLI launcher for weeks; deleting a mechanism that had shipped is a bigger change than tuning a constant."
    counter_response: "It had never fired anywhere ([CXC-ICL-COLD], 2026-08-11 status: `main()` gated it on an unset `META_MANIFEST`, so even `BOOTSTRAP=1` injected nothing), and its first live firing is the defect being recorded here. There is no track record to preserve."
    invalidating_assumption: "That `claude`'s positional prompt is accepted in INTERACTIVE mode (not only under `-p`) and is delivered to the session rather than discarded when startup gates intervene. `--help` documents `claude [options] [command] [prompt]` with no `-p` qualifier, but the ORDERING against startup dialogs is unmeasured. If a positional prompt turns out to be swallowed by a gate, the fallback is NOT the settle delay — it is a harness-level structured input (tier 1) or an explicit human 'orient' affordance in the widget, because typing at startup stays banned either way."
- upholds: [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS], [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY]
- supersedes: [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]
- relates-to: [CXC-ICL-COLD], [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-MIDSESSION-SEAM] `{"cmd":"compact"}` and `{"cmd":"inject"}` STAY — mid-session actuation is a DIFFERENT seam from startup

- definition: two seams write to a hosted child, and they are not variants of one thing.

| Seam | Target state | Mechanism | Status |
|------|--------------|-----------|--------|
| STARTUP | a child that has not yet drawn a TUI, possibly sitting on a human-gated dialog | was: a timed PTY injection | **DELETED** — the prompt rides argv ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]) |
| MID-SESSION | a LIVE TUI with a working entry area, reading normally | `{"cmd":"compact"}` / `{"cmd":"inject"}` on `intelligence/<id>/out`, through the three-phase clear/type/submit | **KEPT, UNCHANGED** — it works today and is load-bearing |

- requirement: (David's ruling, explicit) the startup deletion MUST NOT touch the mid-session
  path. `terminal.py:521` subscribes and `terminal.py:784` handles; the three-phase actuation
  ([CXC-TERM-CONVERGE], [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]) is unchanged in both hosts.
- rationale: (why they LOOK like one mechanism and are not) both end in bytes on the same fd
  through the same lock, so a builder deleting "the injection path" can plausibly delete both.
  What distinguishes them is the STATE they target: mid-session has a reader that is definitely
  ready, so no timing assumption is being made and there is nothing unsound to remove. The
  startup seam's defect was never the writing — it was asserting readiness with a timer.
- constraint: `/compact` self-direction is the entire reason the harness exists
  ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE], and the capability that DISQUALIFIED headless mode,
  [CXC-TERM-REJECT-HEADLESS]). Removing this seam would remove the reason the widget is a host
  rather than a viewer.
- scenario:
  - when: the startup-injection deletion lands
  - then: a peer publishing `{"cmd":"inject","text":"hello"}` still produces byte-identical
    output to the pre-deletion path, and a self-directed `/compact` still fires
- verify-machine: a byte-identity test for both commands across the deletion commit; a grep
  guard that the `out`-topic subscription and its handler still exist and have exactly one
  call site per host.
- verify-human: David asks the hosted instance to inject text after the deletion and sees it
  appear as if he had typed it ([CXC-TERM-ACCEPT] TERM-C8, unchanged).
- upholds: [CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE]
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-CONVERGE], [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED] SUPERSEDED — the fixed settle delay, kept with its evidence rather than deleted silently

- status: superseded
- definition: (what it was, stated so the record is readable without a `git log`) from
  2026-08-11 until the same day's live spawn, the cold-start bootstrap was typed into the child
  through the PTY after a FIXED settle delay — `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY_S = 3.0`, overridable by
  `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY`, armed by a `threading.Timer` in BOTH hosts (`Launcher.arm_bootstrap`,
  `terminal.py::_arm_bootstrap`).
- rationale: (what it was built on — it was not careless, which is why it is worth keeping) a readiness
  DETECTOR would have to watch the child's output, and output pattern-matching at startup is
  banned PERMANENTLY ([CXC-TERM-DIALOGS], [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY]) after an earlier
  auto-answering launcher collided with the human and hung. Given a choice between a banned
  detector and a fixed delay, the fixed delay was the conservative option — and it was reused
  deliberately rather than invented, which is why the code says "the EXISTING settle-delay
  mechanism is reused rather than a readiness detector invented".
- evidence: (what RETIRED it — all from the first live spawn, 2026-08-11)

| # | Observation | What it falsified |
|---|-------------|-------------------|
| 1 | the prompt landed in a blocking `Enter y/n:` read (the channel-approval dialog) | that 3.0 s clears the startup gates — it does not, and no fixed value can, because the gate count is human- and grant-dependent |
| 2 | `^[[200~` echoed as LITERAL TEXT | that `?2004h` is on by the time anything is typed — the census figure behind that claim came from profiles with no dialogs in them ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]) |
| 3 | `claude [options] [command] [prompt]` accepts a positional prompt | that typing was necessary AT ALL — the premise under which a delay was the conservative option |

- consequence: rows 1 and 2 alone would have argued for a bigger delay or a better wrapper. Row
  3 is what makes this a DELETION: the choice was never detector-vs-timer, it was
  typing-vs-not-typing, and nobody had checked `--help`.
- lesson: (why this node exists rather than a deleted branch) the delay was defensible under a
  premise nobody re-read. The cheap check that dissolved a whole mechanism — one `--help` — cost
  less than the tuning discussion it replaced. Read the interface before designing around it.
- superseded-by: [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY] The hosted child gets the launcher's EXISTING monitor — thresholds 90 and 95, and the prompt stops lying

- premise: (measured, 2026-08-11) `HarnessMonitor` (`intelligence/launcher/monitor.py`) is
  instantiated ONLY by the launcher — `build_monitor` at `launcher.py:389`, started on a thread
  at `launcher.py:782`. `terminal.py` references it NOWHERE. A widget-hosted child therefore has
  no threshold alerts, no quiescence heartbeat, and no `{"cmd":"context"}` fuel gauge.
- premise: (and the prompt asserts otherwise) `ptycore.py:1079` types into EVERY child —
  "watch self/context_level; at your chosen threshold (~{thr}) PAUSE & REFLECT … The launcher
  will type it." Nothing publishes `self/context_level` for a hosted child, so that instruction
  points at a permanently silent namespace. An instruction to watch a signal that will never
  arrive is worse than no instruction: the instance waits instead of acting.
- requirement: (REUSE BY REFERENCE — do NOT spec a second monitor) the widget runs the SAME
  `HarnessMonitor` class the launcher runs, constructed through the SAME factory. `build_monitor`
  moves from `launcher.py` into the shared core alongside everything else both hosts need
  ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]) — the widget cannot import `launcher.py`, which installs signal handlers
  and takes a superseding flock in `run()`. A second monitor implementation, or a widget-local
  copy of the wiring, is a review reject.
- requirement: (pointed at the CHILD) the monitor's `read_context` resolves the CHILD's
  transcript — the pin the widget already exports before fork ([CXC-TERM-FACTS] fact 1,
  `export_session_pin`) — never the host's. Its `publish_in` delivers to the CHILD's channel
  (`intelligence/<child-id>/in`), and its telemetry lands under `self/<child-id>/*` via the
  EXISTING `self_publisher --namespace-by-id` contract, which already scopes exactly this
  ([CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY], and see [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] for which process runs it).
  A monitor wired to the host's transcript would report the HOST's context to the CHILD, which
  is symptom 2 of [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] rebuilt on purpose.
- requirement: (thresholds are 90 and 95, ONLY — David, 2026-08-11: 65 and 80 were not useful)
  the hosted child's monitor is constructed with `thresholds=(90, 95)`. The DISTINCTION
  `monitor.py` already draws is preserved exactly: 90 is `KIND_FYI` — a heads-up, no action
  required — and 95 is `KIND_GUIDANCE`, the actionable hand-over checklist for a self-directed
  `/compact`, NOT a bare notice. Collapsing 95 into another FYI removes the only message that
  tells the instance what to DO.
- default: `CONTEXT_THRESHOLDS` = `90,95` for a hosted child. The CLI launcher's own default is
  UNCHANGED by this spec; the value is a construction argument, not an edit to
  `HarnessMonitor`'s declared default.
- requirement: (the fuel gauge) `{"cmd":"context"}` on the child's channel is answered by the
  same `monitor.handle_query()` path the launcher uses (`launcher.py:643`). With no monitor the
  widget must answer honestly — "the context monitor is not enabled this session" — exactly as
  `launcher.py:646` already does, rather than silently dropping the command.
- requirement: (correct the prompt — it names the wrong actuator) `ptycore.py:1079`'s "The
  launcher will type it" is FALSE for a widget-hosted child; the HOST types it. The generated
  text MUST name the actuator generically — the HOST that owns this session's PTY — so one
  string is true in both hosts. Naming a specific component in a string both hosts emit is the
  fork this file's builder keeps having to close ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]).
- scenario:
  - when: a hosted child's context crosses 90%
  - then: it receives ONE `context-fyi` on its own channel, and the console's gauge for the
    CHILD's id moves — the host's gauge does not
- scenario:
  - when: it crosses 95%
  - then: it receives `context-compact-guidance` — the hand-over checklist, not a second FYI
- scenario:
  - when: the child publishes `{"cmd":"context"}` and no monitor is enabled
  - then: it gets the explicit "not enabled this session" answer, never silence
- verify-machine: a test asserts the widget constructs a `HarnessMonitor` through the SHARED
  factory with `thresholds == (90, 95)`; a test asserts `read_context` resolves the CHILD's
  jsonl and not the host's; a test asserts the 90 crossing emits `KIND_FYI` and the 95 crossing
  emits `KIND_GUIDANCE`; a grep guard asserting exactly ONE `HarnessMonitor(` construction site
  in the tree; a test asserting no generated prompt contains the string "The launcher will type
  it".
- verify-human: David watches a hosted child's gauge track the CHILD's usage while his own
  Architect's gauge tracks his, and sees the 95 message arrive as a checklist he would act on.
- change: "2026-08-11: the widget wires the launcher's EXISTING HarnessMonitor to the hosted child (thresholds 90/95 only, per David), and `build_monitor` moves to the shared core so there is one construction site. The generated prompt's 'The launcher will type it' is corrected to name the HOST."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "`HarnessMonitor` was constructed only in `launcher.py::build_monitor` (line 389) and started at line 782; `terminal.py` never referenced it. Default thresholds were (65, 80, 90, 95). The bootstrap prompt told every child to watch `self/context_level` and said the launcher would type its `/compact`."
    reasoning: "The capability already exists, is already tested, and already draws the FYI/GUIDANCE distinction that makes 95 actionable. The gap was purely that its one construction site lives in a module a widget structurally cannot import — the same known-hazard [CXC-TERM-PTYCORE] already names — so the fix is an extraction, not a feature."
    rejected_alternative: "Give the widget a small monitor of its own, since it only needs two thresholds — rejected: it forks the hysteresis, the arming/re-arming state machine, the echo grace and the heartbeat cadence, and the fork would drift on the first change to either. Also rejected: leave the child unmonitored and let the console's existing host gauge cover it — that is symptom 2 restated, reporting the host's number under the child's name."
    invalidating_assumption: "That 90 and 95 are the useful crossings for a hosted child specifically. David retired 65 and 80 on his OWN sessions; a hosted child doing short bounded work may want no alerts at all, and a long-running one may want an earlier heads-up. The thresholds are a construction argument precisely so this reopens as a value change, not a redesign."
- uses: [CXC-IC-MONITOR]
- relates-to: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID], [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-WIDGET] The widget — a registry descriptor, a `build` hook, and NO `build_leaf` edit

- definition: a new widget `terminal` (`python/composeio/render/widgets/terminal.py`), registered in
  `WIDGET_REGISTRY` as a `WidgetDescriptor` carrying a `build` callable — the S6 pluggable seam
  ([CXC-ICP-FOUND-B]: "a NEW widget = its module + one descriptor with a `build` hook, no `build_leaf`
  edit"). Using that seam is the reuse-by-reference requirement; adding a `build_leaf` branch is a
  review reject.
- requirement: (the descriptor's placement fields are LOAD-BEARING here) `min_size` = `(560, 380)` —
  40 columns of 12 px Plex Mono plus padding is the floor at which the widget is usable, and the
  console's left rail is **332 px wide**, so the terminal STRUCTURALLY CANNOT be a rail card. The
  descriptor's own fit test is what steers it away, with no surface-specific special case.
- requirement: `region_affinity` = `{"hero", "tab", "dashboard"}` — explicitly NOT `rail`.
- defaults: (every value stated)
  - `role` = `architect` — CARRIED by the scene, never guessed ([CXC-IC-SELF-IDENTITY]). This
    REPLACES the old scalar `intelligence_id` default, which handed the child its host's id and
    caused all three symptoms in [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]. There is no `intelligence_id` default any
    more: the child's id is MINTED per [DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME] and a scene naming the host's
    own id is refused
  - `CONTEXT_THRESHOLDS` (hosted child) = `90,95` ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY])
  - `BOOTSTRAP_ARGV_MAX` = `131072` bytes; there is NO `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` — the knob is deleted
    with the mechanism ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV])
  - `claude_bin` = explicit -> `CLAUDE_BIN` -> `CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH` -> `claude`
    ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-IDENTITY]; the bare-name default alone FAILED on the glass)
  - `autostart` = `false` — the widget mounts IDLE and shows a "no session" plate; a session begins on
    an explicit act ([CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55]). A widget that spawns a child merely by appearing in a
    scene would spawn one on every headless capture
  - `TARGET_MODE` = the value [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE] records from the census; until then this default is
    UNSET and the widget refuses to spawn rather than guessing ([CXC-TERM-PIN])
  - `AX_STARTUP_QUIET_MS` = `0`, whenever `--ax-screen-reader` is the target ([CXC-TERM-AXMODE])
  - `SCROLLBACK_LINES` = `2000`; `TERM_FONT_PX` = `12.0`; `CELL_H` = `15.0`; `PAD` = `8.0`
  - `MAX_BYTES_PER_TICK` = `262144`; `RESIZE_DEBOUNCE_S` = `0.15`; `SEQ_TAIL_MAX` = `64`
  - `TERM` (child env) = `xterm-256color`
  - `captures_tab` = `true` for this widget (`false` engine-wide — [CXC-TERM-INPUT])
  - `focus_ring` = `false`; the widget's own border + block cursor signal focus, matching the console
    composer's opt-out
- requirement: (headless safety) in a headless render the widget MUST NOT spawn a child, MUST draw its
  idle plate, and MUST leave every existing capture byte-identical. This is a hard gate: the render
  capture suite is the instrument that catches a rail that silently reflowed.
- requirement: (unmount tears down) `on_unmount` stops the pump, releases the singleton, closes the
  master fd, and SIGTERMs the child — the same teardown `Launcher.shutdown()` performs. A tab switch
  that leaves an orphaned `claude` holding the singleton is a defect that only shows up an hour later.
- scenario:
  - when: the widget is rendered headlessly
  - then: no `pty.fork` occurs, the idle plate draws, and the capture is byte-identical to before
- scenario:
  - when: the widget is unmounted with a live child
  - then: the child is terminated, the fd closed, and the singleton released
- verify-machine: a registry test asserting the descriptor exists with the stated `min_size` and
  affinity, and that it does NOT fit the 332 px rail; a headless test asserting zero forks; an unmount
  test asserting teardown; the console capture-diff green.
- uses: [CXC-ICP-FOUND-B]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 5. Hazards — the three ways this ships broken

### [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS] The startup-dialog moment — RENDER them, let the human answer them IN THE WIDGET, never auto-answer

- constraint: (permanent, inherited, and non-negotiable) an earlier launcher auto-answered Claude Code's
  startup dialogs by watching PTY output and injecting menu keystrokes. In a real run it COLLIDED WITH
  THE HUMAN AND HUNG. That is bug #2 in `intelligence/launcher/README.md`, the reason the proxy is
  transparent today, and the reason [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY] forbids it "permanently, not deferred".
- requirement: V1 MUST render the startup dialogs in the widget and let the HUMAN answer them there,
  with the keyboard, through the ordinary input path. It MUST NOT detect a dialog, MUST NOT pattern-match
  output for one, and MUST NOT inject any keystroke at startup.
- requirement: (this is the hardest acceptance criterion, and the whole point) the dialogs are the most
  demanding thing V1 renders, because they are the ONE moment where the human must read a specific
  screen and press a specific key. If the census's supported set does not cover them, V1 does not work —
  which is why "startup dialogs render and are answerable" is acceptance TERM-C1 and is glass-gated.
- not-implied: **(the same correction [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY] carries, restated so it is not
  re-conflated)**
  this bans auto-answering DIALOGS. It does NOT ban the re-register BOOTSTRAP for a cold start
  ([CXC-ICL-COLD]) — but as of 2026-08-11 the distinction no longer has to be POLICED, because
  the bootstrap is no longer typed at all: it rides the child's positional prompt argument at
  exec time ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]). This requirement is therefore satisfied BY CONSTRUCTION
  rather than by a carve-out that had to be held correct by timing. The carve-out and the timer
  it protected are both DELETED ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]).
- known-hazard: (unmeasured, and it changes how much this matters) `SKIP_PERMISSIONS` defaults ON, and
  `--dangerously-skip-permissions` "may reduce, but not eliminate, the startup dialogs". WHICH dialogs
  actually still appear under the widget's launch configuration is not measured. The census
  ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS]) MUST capture the startup phase separately for exactly this reason.
- scenario:
  - when: a fresh session starts in the widget and a startup dialog appears
  - then: the dialog is legible, the human answers it with the keyboard, and the session becomes usable
    — with ZERO bytes written by the widget itself before the human's first keypress
- verify-machine: a test asserts that between spawn and the first human input event the widget writes
  ZERO bytes to the master fd — UNCONDITIONALLY, with no exception for a bootstrap timer, since
  there is no longer a timer to except ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]).
- verify-human: David launches a session in the widget, answers the dialogs on the glass, and reaches a
  working prompt. Mandatory, non-delegable.
- upholds: [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE] Redraw cost — scope damage to DIRTY ROWS and coalesce to one feed per frame

- constraint: **(the measured stakes)** a full-scene raster of the 1280x1300 Intelligence Console is
  **~52 ms/frame** (`engine/damage.py`). A terminal streams bytes continuously. A widget that marks full
  damage per chunk would hold the console at ~19 fps forever and make every keystroke laggy —
  [DEC-DAMAGE-FANIN-SCOPE] exists because unscoped bus fan-in damage did exactly that, and David caught
  it on feel while the machine tests were green.
- requirement: **(the intake path, and it is the ONE correct shape)** the PTY read happens on a
  reader
  thread; the emulator is fed on the RENDER thread via `EngineContext.schedule_update(fn, target=self)`
  — the engine's thread-safe handoff, whose `target` argument exists precisely to scope the resulting
  damage to one widget. The widget MUST NOT touch emulator state from the reader thread, and MUST NOT
  call `mark_dirty` / `add_full`.
- requirement: **(coalescing)** the reader thread appends to a byte queue. AT MOST ONE feed per
  frame drains
  it, capped at `MAX_BYTES_PER_TICK` (**default 262144**); the remainder carries to the next tick. This
  is what keeps a `cat` of a large file from stalling a frame — an uncapped drain converts a burst into a
  visible freeze.
- requirement: **(dirty-row damage)** the emulator reports which lines changed (`screen.dirty`).
  Damage is
  the union of those rows' rects — `request_redraw(rect)` per band — NOT the widget rect and NEVER the
  scene. A one-line update damages ~15 px, not 1280x1300.
- default: **(the full-rect escalation)** when dirty rows exceed `DIRTY_ROW_FULL_FRAC` (**default
  0.5**) of
  visible rows, damage the whole WIDGET rect in one call — one rect is cheaper than N overlapping ones.
  It never escalates past the widget.
- requirement: **(the latency bar, measurable)**

| Bar | Value | Instrument |
|-----|-------|-----------|
| keystroke -> byte on the master fd | <= 2 ms (p99) | unit timing test, no render |
| keystroke -> visible echo | <= 2 frames (33 ms at the engine's default 60 fps) p95, while output streams at >= 50 KiB/s | frame-counter test under an injected clock |
| terminal draw cost per frame | <= 8 ms for an 80x24 grid (half a frame budget) | draw-timing test on the fixture grid |
| damage area for a one-line update | <= 2 rows' worth of rect | assertion on the recorded damage rects |
- scenario:
  - when: 1 MiB arrives in a single burst
  - then: it is consumed over multiple frames at `MAX_BYTES_PER_TICK` each, no frame exceeds its budget,
    and typing stays responsive throughout
- scenario:
  - when: one line of output changes
  - then: the recorded damage is that line's band, not the widget rect and not the scene
- verify-machine: a damage test asserting the recorded rects for a one-line update; a burst test
  asserting the per-tick cap and that no `add_full` occurs; timing tests for the first, third and fourth
  bars above.
- verify-human: David types into the widget while a long response streams and confirms it feels snappy —
  the same two-tier verdict that closed [DEC-DAMAGE-FANIN-SCOPE], where a green 0.1 ms machine test hid a
  glass-only regression.
- uses: [CXC-R-DAMAGE]
- relates-to: [DEC-DAMAGE-FANIN-SCOPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-DEGRADE] A half-working terminal is WORSE than Terminal.app — every gap must be VISIBLE

- constraint: partial escape-sequence support that renders SUBTLY WRONG is worse than no terminal at
  all, because the human trusts what they read. Every gap MUST be loud.
- requirement: **(the status strip)** the widget renders a one-line status strip (bottom,
  theme-faint) that
  always shows `<cols>x<rows>`, the child's state (`idle` / `running pid N` / `exited N`), and the
  release-chord hint. When the unsupported-sequence counter is non-zero it appends
  `· N unsupported (<top sequence name>)`.
- requirement: **(the DEGRADED state — the hard stop)** observing a sequence in the table's
  **model-breaking** class — alt-screen (`?1049h`, `?47h`), scroll regions (DECSTBM), or `2J` — means
  V1's line-oriented model is WRONG for what the child is doing. The widget MUST enter a DEGRADED state:
  a persistent banner naming the sequence and the reason, the grid dimmed, input still forwarded (so the
  human can `/quit` or fix the mode), and NO claim that the render is correct.
- requirement: **(bus-visible, not just pixel-visible)** the counter and the degraded flag are
  PUBLISHED to
  `intelligence/<id>/terminal/degraded` as `{"degraded": bool, "counts": {...}, "top": "<name>"}`, so a
  headless test, a monitor, and an Intelligence can all see it. A condition visible only in pixels cannot
  be regression-tested.
- requirement: **(mouse tracking is a NAMED gap, not a silent one)** V1 does not send mouse reports.
  If the
  child enables `?1000h`, the widget counts it and names it in the strip — the child simply never sees
  clicks, keyboard still works, and the human is told rather than left wondering why clicking does
  nothing.
- default: `degraded` is sticky for the session once set. A sequence that broke the model once will break
  it again; clearing the banner on the next clean frame would make the failure flicker.
- scenario:
  - when: the child emits a scroll-region sequence
  - then: the banner appears naming DECSTBM, the namespace publishes `degraded: true`, and the widget
    does NOT pretend the render is correct
- scenario:
  - when: the child enables mouse tracking
  - then: the strip names it, no banner appears, and keyboard input is unaffected
- verify-machine: a test per model-breaking sequence asserting the banner, the sticky flag and the
  publish; a test asserting a supported-only stream leaves the counter at zero and the strip clean.
- verify-human: David confirms that when something is unsupported he can TELL, immediately, without
  reading a log.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 6. Errors and boundaries — every one the design implies, written down

### [CXC-TERM-BOUNDARIES] The failure matrix — what happens, and what is VISIBLE, in each

- definition: the enumeration. The "surfaced as" column is normative; there is no row whose behaviour is
  "nothing happens".

| # | Condition | Behaviour | Surfaced as |
|---|-----------|-----------|-------------|
| 1 | Widget rect below `MIN_COLS`x`MIN_ROWS` | NO spawn; TOO SMALL plate | the plate, with the actual and required dimensions |
| 2 | `claude` binary missing / `execvp` fails | child exits 127; widget shows `exited 127` + the exec error the child wrote | status strip + the grid's last bytes |
| 3 | Child exits normally | grid FROZEN with its final content (not cleared), strip reads `exited <code>`, a "start a new session" affordance appears | strip + grid |
| 4 | Child killed by a signal | as row 3, strip reads `exited (signal N)` | strip |
| 5 | Master fd EOF with the child still running | pump stops, teardown runs, strip reads `disconnected` | strip |
| 6 | Singleton superseded by another launcher ([CXC-TERM-SINGLETON]) | relay stops, child torn down, DEGRADED banner "superseded by another launcher for `<id>`" | banner |
| 7 | Model-breaking sequence observed ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]) | sticky DEGRADED banner; input still forwarded | banner + `terminal/degraded` |
| 8 | Unsupported but non-breaking sequence | counted, dropped, named in the strip | strip counter |
| 9 | Burst exceeds `MAX_BYTES_PER_TICK` | consumed across frames; no frame over budget | none (correct — it is the designed path) |
| 10 | Resize below the minimum while a child RUNS | child is NOT killed; TOO SMALL plate replaces the grid; `TIOCSWINSZ` is clamped to the minimum, never sent as 0 | the plate |
| 11 | Headless render | no spawn, idle plate, captures byte-identical | none (the no-regression property) |
| 12 | `pyte` import fails (not installed) | widget refuses to spawn and renders "terminal emulator unavailable — `pip install -r requirements.txt`" | the plate; the console keeps running |
| 13 | Bus down | keyboard input and rendering are UNAFFECTED (they are in-process); only the `/compact` relay and the degraded publish are lost | strip shows `bus offline` |
| 14 | Malformed UTF-8 from the child | replacement characters, never a crash and never a dropped chunk | the glyphs themselves |
| 15 | Human closes the console with a live child | `on_unmount` teardown (row 5's path); the child is SIGTERMed, not orphaned | none |
| 16 | The configured id equals the HOST's own id ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]) | NO spawn | refusal plate naming BOTH ids and where each came from |
| 17 | Manifest chain exhausted — no `<id>` and no `<role>` manifest ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]) | NO spawn | refusal plate naming every path tried IN ORDER, plus `intelligence/<child-id>/terminal/refusal` |
| 18 | Same as row 17 but `BOOTSTRAP=0` is set | child spawns UNORIENTED, deliberately — the explicit human opt-out | strip reads `unoriented (BOOTSTRAP=0)`, so it is never mistaken for an oriented session |
| 19 | Built bootstrap prompt exceeds `BOOTSTRAP_ARGV_MAX` | NO spawn; never a truncated exec | refusal plate naming the size and the limit |
| 20 | Another live instance already owns the child's channel id ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE]) | the child's bridge REFUSES the channel — it does NOT displace the incumbent; the session still runs, unchannelled | banner "channel id `<id>` is held by another instance" + the bridge's announcement on `intelligence/<id>/channel/contested` |
| 21 | ⌘V with an empty or unavailable clipboard | zero bytes written; no marker pair ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]) | none (correct — it is the designed path) |
| 22 | `{"cmd":"context"}` with no monitor enabled | answered "the context monitor is not enabled this session", never dropped ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]) | the channel reply + `no monitor` in the strip |
| 23 | Chain leg 3 resolves a ROLE manifest whose `id` differs from the child's instance id ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]) | the child is oriented from the role's artifacts and NAMED by its INSTANCE id; the manifest's `id` is used for nothing but the fallback — this is the designed path, not a failure | none on the glass (correct); the assertion is TERM-C19's exact string on the prompt's identity line |
- constraint: rows 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 12, 16, 17 and 19 all leave the widget immediately RE-LAUNCHABLE. A failure that
  leaves the surface permanently inert is worse than the Terminal.app dependency it replaced.
- constraint: **(row 13 is the honesty check)** the terminal must keep working with the bus down,
  because the
  PTY is in-process. A design in which a bus outage breaks typing has put something in the wrong place.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 7. Acceptance — a machine signal AND, for every human-perceptible surface, a glass gate

### [CXC-TERM-ACCEPT] The acceptance criteria

- definition: two-tier by construction — a machine signal is necessary and, for any surface a human
  perceives, never sufficient. TERM-C1..C6 map one-to-one onto David's six stated criteria (C2 is the
  census, C3 the `/compact` relay, C7 the transcript, C8 the convergence, C9 the latency bar).
  TERM-C13..C18 were added 2026-08-11 and each maps one-to-one onto a FINDING of the first live
  spawn — C13 the bootstrap delivery, C14 the shared identity, C15 the manifest chain, C16 the
  missing monitor, C17 the clipboard, C18 the census's configuration fidelity.

| ID | Criterion | when / then | verify.machine | verify.human (glass) |
|----|-----------|-------------|----------------|----------------------|
| TERM-C1 | A real `claude` session runs INSIDE the widget | WHEN a session is started THEN startup dialogs render, the human answers them in-widget, and the session becomes usable | zero widget-originated bytes before the first human keypress ([CXC-TERM-DIALOGS]); a fixture-stream golden of the dialog screen | **David starts a session in the widget, answers the dialogs on the glass, and works in it.** Mandatory, non-delegable |
| TERM-C2 | The census exists; V1's TARGET MODE and supported set are both DERIVED from it | WHEN the census runs THEN all four profiles are captured under the three validity conditions, they DIFFER measurably, and both the target and the supported set are generated | the committed census tool + report covering `tui: fullscreen` / `tui: default` / `--ax-screen-reader` / the combination; the three validity assertions (prompt reached, steady state, resize segment); the generated [CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE]; the tool's selftest asserting it ABORTS on a no-prompt capture and on identical profiles | David reads the report and it matches what he sees; David ratifies the chosen target ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]) |
| TERM-C3 | `/compact <direction>` from the relay fires, exactly as through a real terminal | WHEN the instance emits a compact directive THEN the widget's child receives the identical three-phase clear/type/submit | a test drives `on_out_message` against the widget-hosted core and asserts byte-identical output to the launcher path | David watches a real self-directed compaction happen inside the widget |
| TERM-C4 | Anything outside the supported set degrades VISIBLY | WHEN an unsupported sequence arrives THEN it is counted and named; a model-breaking one raises the sticky banner | per-sequence tests for the strip, the banner, the sticky flag and the publish ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]) | David can TELL something is unsupported without reading a log |
| TERM-C5 | No auto-answered dialogs, ever | WHEN a session starts THEN the widget injects nothing | the zero-bytes assertion + a grep guard that no output-pattern-matching dialog detector exists | — |
| TERM-C6 | An out-of-scope mode is refused HONESTLY, not rendered badly | WHEN the child runs a mode V1 does not target THEN the degraded banner appears | the `?1049h` test ([CXC-TERM-SCOPE]); a test asserting the widget refuses to spawn with `TARGET_MODE` unset rather than guessing | David sets the target to fullscreen and sees a banner, not a mess |
| TERM-C7 | The session transcript is still written | WHEN a widget-hosted session runs THEN its `.jsonl` exists and the console data layer reads THAT one | a test asserts `COMPOSEIO_SESSION_JSONL` is exported before fork and that `resolve_session_jsonl` returns it | David's context gauge tracks the widget-hosted session |
| TERM-C8 | Bus keystrokes and keyboard keystrokes are indistinguishable to the child | WHEN both paths deliver the same text THEN the child receives identical bytes | byte-identical assertion across both paths + the single-write-site grep guard ([CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]) | David asks the Intelligence to inject text and sees it appear as if he typed it |
| TERM-C9 | Typing stays responsive under streaming output | WHEN output streams at >= 50 KiB/s THEN the four bars in [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE] hold | the four timing/damage tests | **David types during a long streaming response and it feels snappy** — the [DEC-DAMAGE-FANIN-SCOPE] verdict, which a green machine test previously failed to catch |
| TERM-C10 | Tab reaches the child; every other widget is unchanged | WHEN Tab is pressed in the terminal THEN `\t` is sent and focus does not move; in any other widget focus still moves | the focus tests ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]) | David uses Tab in-widget and Tab-navigates the rest of the console |
| TERM-C11 | One PTY implementation in the tree | WHEN the guard tests run THEN `pty.fork(` and the select pump appear exactly once | the grep guard + the launcher selftest green post-extraction ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]) | — |
| TERM-C12 | Nothing regresses | WHEN the slice lands THEN every existing capture is byte-identical and no test regresses | the render capture-diff green; `pytest intelligence/` + the render suite green with no unexplained drop in collected count | David bounces the console and sees no visual change where no terminal is placed |
| TERM-C13 | The bootstrap arrives WITHOUT a keystroke | WHEN a cold session spawns THEN the prompt is on the child's argv and ZERO bytes reach the master fd before the human's first keypress | the argv assertion (last element is the prompt, first line names the absolute manifest path); the UNCONDITIONAL zero-bytes real-spawn test; grep guards that `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` / `arm_bootstrap` / `_type_bootstrap` are gone; the `{"cmd":"compact"}`+`{"cmd":"inject"}` byte-identity test ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]) | **David cold-starts a session in the widget, answers whatever dialogs appear, and the instance arrives ORIENTED — no `^[[200~` on the glass, nothing typed under his hands.** Mandatory, non-delegable |
| TERM-C14 | One id, one instance — a hosted child never displaces its host | WHEN the widget spawns a child THEN the child's id differs from the host's and the host keeps its channel, its status and its transcript | the distinct-id test; the host-bridge-still-alive test; the lock-name and telemetry-namespace assertions ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]) | David runs a hosted child and his own Architect keeps answering on the console |
| TERM-C15 | An unoriented instance is never indistinguishable from an oriented one | WHEN the manifest chain is exhausted THEN there is no fork, and the refusal is on the glass AND on the bus | the four-leg chain test; the zero-fork + refusal-publish test; the `BOOTSTRAP=0` labelled-unoriented test ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]) | David removes the role manifest and cannot miss the refusal |
| TERM-C16 | The hosted child has its OWN fuel gauge | WHEN the child crosses 90% then 95% THEN it gets an FYI then the hand-over GUIDANCE on its own channel, and `self/<child-id>/*` moves while the host's does not | the `thresholds == (90, 95)` assertion; the FYI-vs-GUIDANCE kind tests; the child-transcript `read_context` test; the one-`HarnessMonitor(`-construction-site grep ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]) | David watches the child's gauge track the CHILD, and reads a 95 message he would actually act on |
| TERM-C17 | ⌘V pastes through the ONE existing path | WHEN ⌘V is pressed THEN the bytes are identical to the same text delivered through `on_text` | the byte-identity test; the empty-clipboard zero-bytes test; the other-Cmd-chords-still-swallowed test ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]) | David copies a multi-line block from another app and it arrives as ONE prompt |
| TERM-C18 | The census measures the configuration actually run | WHEN the census runs THEN an AS-DEPLOYED profile — real `.mcp.json`, dev-channels flag, permission prompts NOT suppressed — is captured and its STARTUP segment reported separately | the as-deployed argv assertion (no `--strict-mcp-config`, no empty `mcpServers`, no `skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt`); the grep guard that no startup citation omits its profile ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]) | David reads the as-deployed startup capture and recognizes the dialogs he actually answers |
| TERM-C19 | The bootstrap prompt names the INSTANCE, never the manifest it read | WHEN a child is oriented from a ROLE manifest whose `id` differs from the instance id it was spawned under THEN the prompt tells it the INSTANCE's name | **exact string**: `build_bootstrap_prompt(role_manifest, instance_id="architect-hosted")` over a manifest carrying `"id": "composeux-architect"` produces a prompt whose identity line is exactly `You are the Intelligence peer "architect-hosted"`, AND the string `composeux-architect` does NOT appear as the peer identity anywhere in it; the same two assertions again through `build_bootstrap_for` so chain leg 3 is exercised end to end; the fallback test (no `instance_id` -> `manifest["id"]`); and the fixture's manifest `id` MUST DIFFER from the instance id, or the test re-tests the coincidence that hid this ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]) | David cold-starts a child under a role with no per-instance manifest and it introduces itself, and answers on the channel, by its INSTANCE id |
- note: TERM-C19 was added 2026-08-11 (fourth pass) and maps one-to-one onto tonight's finding — chain leg 3 had never once been exercised with a real ROLE manifest, so the manifest-derived name and the instance-derived name agreed by coincidence and no test could tell them apart ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED], [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]).
- constraint: TERM-C1, TERM-C9 and TERM-C13 CANNOT be waived by a green machine run. Every mechanism here can pass
  in isolation while the session is unusable for a reason only a human at the glass will see — a dialog
  that rendered illegibly, a cursor in the wrong cell, a wrap one column early, an input lag that reads
  as a hang.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 8. Sequencing, and what this does to the console-launch spec

### [CXC-TERM-SLICES] Five slices, measurement first, smallest blast radius after

- definition: each slice is independently landable and revertable and ends with the criteria that apply
  to it.

| Slice | Content | Why here | Revert |
|-------|---------|----------|--------|
| 0 | The census tool: the onboarding/prompt assertion, the winsize fix, steady state + a resize segment, all FOUR candidate profiles, a discriminating report, the chosen target ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]) and the generated sequence table ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS], [CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR], [CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE]). NO widget. | It is a MEASUREMENT that has already failed TWICE, and every downstream decision — including which mode V1 even targets — depends on it. If the hypothesis is refuted, the design changes before a line of renderer is written. Ends with TERM-C2. | delete one tool |
| 1 | The PTY-core extraction ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]) with the launcher selftest green and zero behaviour change. NO widget. | A pure refactor with an existing selftest as its gate — the safest possible first code change, and it proves the reuse boundary is real before anything depends on it. Ends with TERM-C11. | one move commit |
| 2 | The emulator + renderer + winsize, driven by a RECORDED byte stream (no live `claude`): grid, colours, cursor, scrollback, damage, degradation ([CXC-TERM-GRID], [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE], [CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]). | Everything visual becomes testable against a fixture, deterministically, before a real child is involved. Ends with TERM-C4, C6, C9. | delete the widget module |
| 3 | Input: the keymap, the `captures_tab` engine opt-out, the release chord, bracketed paste ([CXC-TERM-INPUT], [CXC-TERM-PASTE]). | The only slice that touches the ENGINE (one `getattr` in `focus.py`), so it lands alone and is trivially revertable. Ends with TERM-C10. | revert one focus.py line |
| 4 | Live: spawn, singleton, session pin, the `/compact` relay, teardown ([CXC-TERM-SINGLETON], [CXC-TERM-PTYCORE]). Then the glass run. | The loop is not closed until a real instance runs in the widget and compacts itself. Ends with TERM-C1, C3, C5, C7, C8, C12. | disable `autostart`; the widget degrades to a plate |
| 5 | The FIRST-LIVE-SPAWN corrections, IN THIS ORDER: (a) the distinct child id + the manifest chain ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID], [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]); (b) the channel's refuse-don't-displace rule ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE], a change in spec 34); (c) the argv bootstrap and the DELETION of the settle path ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]); (d) the monitor wiring ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]); (e) ⌘V ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]) and the as-deployed census profile ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]). | Slices 0-4 landed and the widget hosted a real `claude` for the first time; these are what that spawn returned. The ORDER is load-bearing between (a) and (b): the id fix makes a collision RARE, the channel fix makes it AUDIBLE when the rarity fails — landing (b) first would leave a live host displaceable by the next scene copy-paste, and landing (a) alone would leave the eviction silent. (c) is independently landable and is the one the human FELT. Ends with TERM-C13..C18. | each item reverts alone; (c) reverts to a refusal to spawn, NEVER to the deleted timer |
- constraint: slice 0 GATES everything. Building a renderer before the census is building against
  [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS], which is explicitly not measured.
- constraint: slice 2 does not land without a CLEAN capture-diff, for the same reason [CXC-ICL-SLICES]
  gates its UI slice: the console renders perfectly while the data is wrong, and a pixel diff is the only
  instrument that sees it.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55] What this retires in [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] — and the recommended sequencing (do NOT block 55 on this)

- recommendation: **(sequencing)** LAND [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] AS SPECCED FIRST. It is smaller,
  already
  ratified, and closes the "the harness is not the default path" loop NOW. This spec's slice 0 is a
  measurement that may take a day and may refute its own hypothesis; blocking a ratified, working slice
  behind an unstarted measurement trades a certain win for an uncertain one.
- definition: when the widget lands, `_app/intelligence/open` gains a SECOND consumer — the console
  itself — and the target kind changes from `TERMINAL` to an in-process widget mount. The table below is
  what survives and what retires.

| [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] node | Fate | Why |
|---------------------------|------|-----|
| [CXC-ICL-INTENT] the `_app/intelligence/open` intent, payload, id validation | **SURVIVES UNCHANGED** | the intent is mechanism-agnostic; only its consumer changes |
| [CXC-ICL-PRESENCE], [CXC-ICL-VISIBILITY], [CXC-ICL-PANE-SWAP], [CXC-ICL-PANEL] | **SURVIVE UNCHANGED** | the presence rule and the pane swap do not care what the press spawns |
| [CXC-ICL-PEER-GUARD] never launch beside a live instance | **SURVIVES** | still required, and now ALSO enforced in-process by [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON] |
| [CXC-ICL-COLD] cold start with the bootstrap, [CXC-ICL-CWD] instance home | **SURVIVE UNCHANGED** | the widget sets the same env and the same cwd |
| [CXC-ICL-TERMINAL] the `osascript` `do script` spawn + AppleScript escaping + the id-as-shell-word injection surface | **RETIRED** | there is no shell string to build; the id is a Python argument |
| [CXC-ICL-SPAWN-ONCE]'s `LaunchHandle` + `LAUNCH_SETTLE_S` | **RETIRED** | the handle existed ONLY because `osascript` returns in milliseconds so its `poll()` lies. The console owns a real long-lived child pid whose `poll()` is honest, so `SpawnedWindows` works with the actual process handle and the 90 s human-latency timeout disappears |
| [CXC-ICL-ENTRY] `launch-instance.sh` | **DEMOTED, not deleted** | it stays the hand-runnable path ([CXC-ICL-ENTRY] "usable by hand, identically") and the non-darwin fallback; it is no longer the console's mechanism |
| the macOS-only constraint + the `no terminal-open on this platform` refusal | **RETIRED** | the widget runs wherever the engine runs |
- consequence: **(the simplification worth naming)** the widget deletes an entire class of
  workaround — a
  shell-string builder, an injection guard, a lying process handle, a human-latency timeout, and a
  platform refusal — and replaces them with a child process the console actually owns. That is the test
  of whether this was the right layer.
- constraint: retiring those nodes is a `supersedes` edit to [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] made when this widget
  is PROVEN on the glass (TERM-C1), not when it is written. Two launch mechanisms coexisting for a
  release is fine; deleting the working one before the new one is glass-green is not.
- supersedes: [CXC-ICL-TERMINAL]
- relates-to: [DD-TERM-55-SEQUENCING]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [CXC-TERM-OUT] Explicitly out of scope — named so it is not smuggled in

- out: `tui: fullscreen` rendering. V2, with a stated migration path ([CXC-TERM-SCOPE]).
- out: supporting MORE THAN ONE target mode in V1. One target, chosen from the census
  ([DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]); every other mode degrades visibly.
- out: switching the renderer mode at RUNTIME. `/tui` is refused while background work runs, and this
  widget's sessions dispatch background agents routinely ([CXC-TERM-PIN]). The mode is fixed at spawn.
- out: auto-answering startup dialogs, by keystroke injection or any other means ([CXC-TERM-DIALOGS]).
  Permanently out, not deferred — inherited from [CXC-ICL-CONSTRAINT-PTY].
- out: mouse reporting to the child (`?1000h` family). Counted and NAMED in V1, never sent
  ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]).
- out: text SELECTION and copy-out of the grid, and therefore ⌘C. Real, wanted, and a separate
  slice — it needs a selection model neither the widget nor the engine has, and ⌘C without one
  is the selection slice with its visible half removed. It lands WITH selection or not at all.
  Paste-IN (⌘V) is IN, because it is one branch on an input path that already exists
  ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]).
- out: hosting more than one session per widget, tabs of sessions, or a session picker. One widget, one
  child.
- out: any change to the `/compact` actuation relay, its three-phase injection, or the harness monitor's
  own semantics ([CXC-IC-LIFECYCLE], [CXC-IC-MONITOR]). This spec CARRIES them into a new host and changes
  nothing about what they do — [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY] wires the EXISTING monitor to the child and
  chooses its thresholds as construction arguments; it does not redefine the monitor, and a second monitor
  implementation is a review reject.
- out: the STARTUP-injection path in every form — the settle delay, a bigger settle delay, a
  configurable one, or a readiness detector. Deleted, not deferred ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV],
  [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]). MID-SESSION actuation (`{"cmd":"compact"}`,
  `{"cmd":"inject"}`) is a different seam and stays.
- out: the browser tier. The widget is engine-portable by construction, but a browser PTY needs a
  server-side child and a websocket byte channel — a different spec.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## 9. Design decisions

### [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE] Which mode V1 targets — `tui: default` vs `--ax-screen-reader` vs the combination

- decision: **OPEN — deliberately deferred to the census** ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS]). This is the one decision
  in this spec that MUST NOT be settled by reasoning, because the whole point of the census is to settle
  it by measurement. `tui: fullscreen` is the only candidate ruled out in advance, and only for V1.
- rationale: the choice turns on two axes — the size of the demanding sequence set (smaller is better)
  and the amount of shared state the launch touches (less is better). What the evidence would have to
  show to pick each:

| Candidate | The case for it | The case against |
|-----------|-----------------|------------------|
| `--ax-screen-reader` | A per-invocation FLAG: no settings composition, no `--settings` file written to the instance home, and David's global `tui: fullscreen` is untouched — [CXC-TERM-PIN] disappears entirely. Its documented output ("flat text, no decorative borders or animations") is the closest thing to a stated promise of a small sequence set | "Flat text" is a claim about DECORATION, not the control plane; it may still use colour, cursor movement and line editing. Also the least-travelled path — the mode David does not run, so glass-level bugs in it are least likely to be already known |
| `tui: default` | The ordinary interactive TUI, one step down from what David runs daily, so its behaviour is the best-understood of the three | Lives in `~/.claude/settings.json`, so every launch must compose and write a settings file ([CXC-TERM-PIN]) — shared state between the widget path and every terminal session |
| both together | If they are orthogonal, the union may be smaller than either alone | Their interaction is entirely UNMEASURED ([CXC-TERM-AXMODE]) — it may be that one silently overrides the other |
- recommendation: **(weak, and explicitly not a substitute for the measurement)** if the census
  shows the
  two candidates' sequence sets are comparable, prefer `--ax-screen-reader` on the shared-state argument
  alone — it deletes [CXC-TERM-PIN]'s entire settings-composition requirement, and a mechanism that
  needs no shared state is the one that survives contact with a second caller.
- status: proposed — awaiting the census, then David's ratify (GATES slices 2, 3 and 4; does not gate
  slice 1)
- alternatives: support ALL of the measured-viable modes in V1 and let the operator pick — rejected for
  V1: each supported mode is a distinct sequence set, a distinct renderer path, and a distinct set of
  goldens, and "which of three modes is this session in?" is exactly the ambiguity that produces a
  subtly-wrong render ([CXC-TERM-DEGRADE]). ONE target, everything else visibly degraded.
- invalidating_assumption: "That one of the narrow modes has a materially smaller demanding set than
  `tui: fullscreen`. If the census shows all four profiles are substantially the same — full-screen
  addressing everywhere — then there is no narrow target to pick, V1 needs a screen buffer, and this
  decision collapses into 'build the V2 renderer now'. That outcome also reopens
  [DD-TERM-55-SEQUENCING], because 55's Terminal path would then be the answer for much longer."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-SCOPE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-PYTE] `pyte` vs a hand-rolled escape parser vs `pyxtermjs`-style delegation

- decision: PROPOSED (recommended: YES) — depend on `pyte`, pinned, declared in `requirements.txt`
  ([CXC-TERM-EMULATOR]).
- rationale: the escape-sequence state machine is a solved, fiddly, well-tested problem with no domain
  value in re-solving. `pyte` is pure Python (no build step, works on every tier the engine targets),
  has no dependencies of its own, exposes exactly the two things the widget needs (a character grid and
  a dirty-line set), and already implements the alt-screen/scroll-region/absolute-addressing machinery
  V2 will need — so the V1->V2 widening is a renderer change, not an emulator rewrite. A hand-rolled
  parser would be smaller today and would grow the same bugs pyte already fixed.
- alternatives: (a) hand-roll a minimal parser for the census's supported set only — smallest V1, but it
  makes V2 a rewrite and puts this repo in the terminal-emulator business; (b) delegate to a JS terminal
  (xterm.js) behind a web view — mature and complete, but it drags a browser runtime into a Skia app and
  breaks the own-every-pixel property [CXC-RM-CTK-RETIRE] established; (c) `pexpect` — an interaction
  library, not an emulator; it solves the wrong half and `launcher.py` deliberately avoids it.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify
- invalidating_assumption: "That pyte imports and runs correctly on the repo's pinned interpreter
  (macOS, Python 3.13) and that its maintenance state is adequate for a dependency in the harness's
  critical path. If it does not import cleanly under 3.13, or if the census's supported set turns out to
  be small enough that pyte is 90% unused weight, alternative (a) reopens — and the census
  ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS]) is exactly the measurement that decides it. Verify BEFORE writing the renderer."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-EMULATOR]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-TAB-CAPTURE] A `captures_tab` widget opt-out vs re-ordering the focus manager vs a modal input mode

- decision: PROPOSED — a `getattr`-guarded `captures_tab` attribute (default False) consulted by
  `FocusManager.handle_key` before its Tab branch, plus a mandatory release chord ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]).
- rationale: it mirrors an idiom the engine ALREADY has — `focus_ring = False` is consulted with exactly
  this `getattr` pattern so a widget whose own chrome signals focus can opt out. One more attribute on
  the same seam changes no existing widget's behaviour, is one line in `focus.py`, and is revertable
  independently (slice 3). The release chord is what keeps the capture from becoming a keyboard trap.
- alternatives: (a) route Tab to the focused widget FIRST and let it return "unconsumed" — more correct
  in general, but it changes the key path for every widget in the console and cannot be reverted in one
  line; (b) a modal "terminal has grabbed the keyboard" state with an explicit enter/leave — the honest
  full answer, and the right shape if a second capture-hungry widget ever appears, but it is a state
  machine for one widget today; (c) do not send Tab at all — rejected, Claude Code's TUI uses it and a
  terminal missing a key is the subtly-broken class this spec forbids.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify
- invalidating_assumption: "That exactly ONE widget ever needs to capture navigation keys. If a second
  does (a code editor, a game surface, a vim-style pane), the per-widget boolean becomes a set of
  ad-hoc flags and alternative (b)'s explicit input-grab mode should replace it — the same de-fork move
  [CXC-TERM-SEQTABLE] applies to the sequence table."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-INPUT]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-HOST-SURFACE] WHERE the terminal widget lives — this is UNRESOLVED and needs David

- decision: OPEN — the design brief says "inside a ComposeApp" and does not name the surface. This spec
  cannot resolve it, because it is a product call about the console's shape.
- the-constraint-that-narrows-it: the widget's floor is `min_size (560, 380)` ([CXC-TERM-WIDGET])
  and
  the console's left rail is **332 px wide**. The terminal STRUCTURALLY CANNOT be a rail card. It needs
  a hero region, a whole tab, or a dashboard cell.
- alternatives: (a) **a new console TAB** ("Session") — the most room, the least disruption to existing
  layout, and the console already has a tab container ([SPEC:CXC-TAB], `console_tabs.py`);
  RECOMMENDED; (b) the hero region of the existing Collaboration tab, replacing or splitting the chat
  thread — most integrated, but it competes with the surface David actually uses to talk; (c) a
  dashboard cell ([CXC-ICP-FOUND-B] S5) — composable and user-placeable, but a dashboard cell is
  typically too small for 80 columns; (d) its OWN window, launched by `app_launcher` as a second
  ComposeApp — closest to today's mental model, keeps the console uncluttered, and is trivially placeable
  beside the console ([DEC-LAUNCH-PLACEMENT] already publishes the console's bbox for exactly this).
- rationale: (a) is recommended because a tab costs no existing pixels, is reachable in one click,
  survives a console resize, and does not force a decision about the chat thread. (d) is the honest
  runner-up and should be re-considered if David wants the session visible ALONGSIDE the console rather
  than instead of a tab.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify (BLOCKS slice 2's scene wiring; does NOT block slices 0 or 1)
- invalidating_assumption: "That one terminal at a time is enough. If the fleet case ([CXC-IH-CONSOLE])
  arrives and David wants several sessions visible at once, (a) becomes a tab OF tabs and (d) becomes
  the natural answer — one window per instance, placed by the existing bbox mechanism."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-WIDGET]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-55-SEQUENCING] Land [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] first, or wait for the widget?

- decision: PROPOSED (recommended) — land [SPEC:CXC-IC-LAUNCH] as specced; retire its terminal-spawn
  mechanism only when this widget is GLASS-GREEN ([CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55]).
- rationale: 55 is ratified, small, and closes a real gap today; this spec's first slice is a
  measurement that has already failed once and whose hypothesis may be refuted. Blocking a certain win
  on an uncertain one is the trade this recommendation refuses. The cost of landing both is bounded and
  named: one `TARGET_TABLE` row, one `_terminal_argv` change, and a `LaunchHandle` — all of which the
  table in [CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55] marks for retirement, so nothing is written that is not already
  accounted for.
- alternatives: (a) pause 55 and go straight to the widget — avoids writing code destined for deletion,
  but leaves the harness non-default for however long the census + four slices take, which is the exact
  cost [CXC-ICL-THESIS] exists to end; (b) land 55 and never retire the Terminal path, keeping it as the
  non-darwin fallback — defensible, and in fact [CXC-ICL-ENTRY] is demoted rather than deleted for
  precisely this reason.
- status: proposed — awaiting ratify
- invalidating_assumption: "That the census confirms [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS] and V1 is a small build. If
  the census REFUTES it — `tui: default` turns out to be a screen-buffer TUI — the widget becomes a
  substantially larger effort, and 55's Terminal path stops being a bridge and becomes the answer for
  much longer. Re-read this decision the day the census lands."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-SUPERSEDES-55]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME] HOW the hosted child's distinct id is minted — scene-DECLARED vs spawn-MINTED

- decision: OPEN. David's ruling settled the PROPERTY — a hosted child carries an id distinct
  from its host's ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]) — and did not settle the SCHEME. Both branches below
  satisfy the ruling and the manifest chain; they differ in who bears the burden and in what
  goes wrong when it is not borne.
- rationale: the axes are (i) can a collision happen at all, (ii) is the id PREDICTABLE enough to
  address (`intelligence/<id>/in` is a topic a human and a peer both have to be able to type),
  and (iii) can leg 2 of the manifest chain (`<id>.meta.json`) ever hit for a hosted child.

| Branch | The case for it | The case against |
|--------|-----------------|------------------|
| (a) **scene-DECLARED**: the scene carries `role` plus an explicit distinct `instance_id`; the spawn refuses if it equals the host's | Ids stay human-readable and PREDICTABLE, so `intelligence/architect-review/in` is a topic David can type and a peer can bind. A curated per-instance manifest is possible, so chain leg 2 can actually hit. Nothing is generated, so nothing has to be discovered from a log | Puts the burden on every scene author, and the failure mode is the one that ALREADY happened: a copy-pasted scene re-collides. The refusal catches it, but only at spawn time, and only for the HOST's id — two sibling widgets in one scene could still share |
| (b) **spawn-MINTED**: the spawn derives `<role>-<short-uuid>` (or a per-role counter) and the scene carries only the role | A collision becomes structurally impossible, including between siblings, with no scene discipline at all — the property is guaranteed rather than checked | The id is unpredictable, so the child's channel topic cannot be typed or bound ahead of time and must be discovered from the running console; chain leg 2 can never hit, so every hosted child orients from the ROLE manifest and per-instance curation stops being expressible |
- recommendation: (weak, and explicitly not a substitute for David's call) (a) with the refusal
  widened to cover SIBLING widgets in the same scene, not only the host — predictability is what
  makes `intelligence/<id>/*` an address rather than a handle, and an address a human cannot type
  degrades the whole control-is-a-peer property ([CXC-TERM-CONVERGE]). If the fleet case
  ([CXC-IH-CONSOLE]) arrives first, (b) becomes the natural answer and the addressing problem has
  to be solved anyway by a roster.
- alternatives: (c) a HYBRID — scene-declared when present, spawn-minted otherwise. Rejected as
  the default answer because "the id is whatever the scene didn't say" is exactly the
  sometimes-predictable surface that makes an operator guess; it is worth revisiting only if (a)
  proves too burdensome in practice.
- decision: RESOLVED (David, 2026-08-11) — **(a) scene-DECLARED**, with the refusal WIDENED to
  cover sibling collisions exactly as recommended. The scene carries `role` plus an explicit
  `instance_id`; there is no `intelligence_id` scalar and NO default id, so a scene that omits
  `instance_id` produces a widget that refuses and says so rather than falling back to
  `"architect"` — which is the default that caused the defect.
- consequence: (as BUILT) `TerminalWidget(role=…, instance_id=…)`; `intelligence_id` is a
  read-only property returning the child's id, so every namespace keys on one accessor. Three
  refusals fire BEFORE `pty.fork()`: no `instance_id`, the HOST's id (`INTELLIGENCE_ID`), and a
  SIBLING widget mounted in this process already wearing it. The sibling registry is claimed on
  MOUNT and released on UNMOUNT, so two terminals in one scene collide while a test that merely
  constructs widgets does not.
- status: decided
- invalidating_assumption: "That a hosted child is a long-lived, addressable peer. If hosted
  children turn out to be short-lived task runners nobody addresses by hand, predictability buys
  nothing and (b) wins outright — the scheme should then be re-decided against the observed
  lifetime, not against this argument."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] WHICH process publishes `self/<child-id>/*` — an in-process monitor thread vs the existing standalone publisher

- decision: OPEN. [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY] settles WHAT runs (the launcher's existing
  `HarnessMonitor`, thresholds 90/95) and WHERE it points (the child's transcript). It does not
  settle which PROCESS carries the child's `self/*` publishing, and the two answers have
  genuinely different failure modes.
- rationale: the console already runs in-process data sources under a supervisor
  ([CXC-IC-SINGLETON] RECLAIM-ON-RELEASE), and it already knows how to spawn
  `self_publisher --namespace-by-id` as a standalone. Both are established; neither is obviously
  right for a child whose lifetime is a widget's lifetime.

| Branch | The case for it | The case against |
|--------|-----------------|------------------|
| (a) **in-process**, a monitor thread the widget owns, published through the console's client | Lifetime is EXACTLY the child's: mount spawns it, unmount tears it down, and there is no orphan to reap. No second process, no second flock, no `start-console-daemons` edit. The widget already owns a reader thread, so the concurrency shape is one it has | It puts a per-child publisher inside the render process, where a slow read or a wedged transcript parse costs frames — the class [CXC-TERM-DAMAGE] spends its whole budget avoiding. It also means the child's telemetry DIES with the console even when the child does not |
| (b) **standalone** `self_publisher --namespace-by-id <child-id>`, spawned beside the child | Reuses a publisher that already exists, is already singleton-keyed per id (`self-publisher-<id>`), and is already bounce-safe. Isolation is real: nothing it does can cost a frame. The child's telemetry SURVIVES a console bounce, which is the case the reclaim machinery exists for | A second process per child, whose lifetime must be tied to the child's or it orphans — and an orphaned publisher republishing a dead child's last reading is a stale gauge, the exact dishonesty this corpus keeps closing |
- recommendation: (weak) (b), because the isolation argument is the one the console has already
  paid for twice, and because the orphan risk has a known answer (the same superseding
  singleton + teardown the widget already performs for the child, [CXC-TERM-SINGLETON]).
- alternatives: (c) publish nothing and let the CONSOLE read the child's transcript directly in
  its existing data layer — rejected: it puts transcript parsing for N children on the render
  process's critical path and makes the child's telemetry a console feature rather than the
  child's own signal, which breaks the symmetry that lets a hosted child be a peer.
- decision: RESOLVED (David, 2026-08-11) — **(a) an IN-PROCESS monitor thread**, against the
  weak recommendation for (b). The deciding argument is the one branch (a) states and branch
  (b) has to engineer around: the thread's lifetime is EXACTLY the child's — mount spawns it,
  unmount tears it down — so there is no orphan to reap, no second flock, and no
  `start-console-daemons` edit. An orphaned publisher republishing a dead child's last reading
  is a stale gauge, and this design cannot produce one.
- consequence: (as BUILT) `TerminalWidget._start_monitor` calls the SHARED
  `ptycore.build_monitor` with `thresholds=(90, 95)` and starts `HarnessMonitor.start_thread()`;
  `stop_session` stops it. The child's transcript is resolved LATE (a cold child mints its own
  bounded to the child's own project dir, to files written after our fork, and never to the
  host's pin. Harness messages ride the bus's FOREIGN-channel seam to the raw
  `intelligence/<child-id>/in` topic — the same address `channel.mjs` subscribes.
- status: decided
- invalidating_assumption: "That a hosted child's telemetry is worth its own publisher at all.
  If the child's context is only ever read BY the child (through the monitor's channel messages)
  and never rendered on the console, then `self/<child-id>/*` is unread and the whole branch
  collapses into 'the monitor publishes to the channel and nothing else'. Check whether any
  console surface actually binds a child namespace before building either branch."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

### [DD-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV-FORM] What the argv prompt SAYS — the EXPLICIT manifest-naming form vs the bare `complete bootstrap`

- decision: RESOLVED (David, 2026-08-11) — the EXPLICIT form. The prompt's first line names the
  manifest by absolute path: `Read <abs>/intelligence/meta/<id>.meta.json and complete the
  bootstrap it describes` ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]).
- amended: (2026-08-11, as BUILT) the explicit form is delivered through ONE indirection: argv
  names the PROMPT FILE by absolute path and the prompt file's first line names the MANIFEST by
  absolute path. Both halves of "explicit" survive — nothing is discovered, nothing is implied —
  and the 6 KB body stays off the exec boundary. This does not reopen the decision; it records
  WHERE each absolute path is spelled ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV] `amended`).
- rationale: it is self-contained. There is no discovery step, nothing for the child to infer
  about which manifest is its own, and it still resolves for a child spawned OUTSIDE this repo —
  where a relative path or an implied convention would resolve to nothing or, worse, to some
  other instance's manifest.
- alternatives: the bare `complete bootstrap` — prettier, shorter, and it reads like an
  instruction to a colleague who already knows the ritual. REJECTED because it assumes context
  the child may not have: a cold instance has not read the corpus, does not know which id it is
  wearing, and cannot resolve "the bootstrap" to a file. The prettier form is the one that fails
  precisely in the case the bootstrap exists to serve — an instance that knows nothing yet.
- status: decided
- invalidating_assumption: "That the child can READ the named path. It is an absolute path on
  the spawning host's filesystem; a child that ever runs in a container, on another machine, or
  under a different user loses it silently and boots unoriented. If hosting ever crosses a
  filesystem boundary the prompt must carry the manifest CONTENT (bounded, and against
  [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]'s size limit) rather than a pointer to it — at which point this
  decision reopens as a payload-vs-pointer call, not a wording one."
- documents: [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

