### [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED] The bootstrap prompt DERIVES the wake ritual from its spec — it never restates it

- constraint: `build_bootstrap_prompt` generates the text a cold-started instance receives. The wake
  procedure it must induce is ALREADY RATIFIED in [SPEC:SESSION-BOOTSTRAP-READ]. A generator that
  paraphrases those rules into a Python string is a SECOND IMPLEMENTATION of the ritual whose copy
  drifts silently from the spec — [INSIGHT-FORK-DIVERGENCE-IS-A-CLASS], and the same mistake
  [SBR-PATHS-SINGLE-SOURCE] already forbids in its path-literal form.
- requirement: the prompt CITES the ritual — the spec id, [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW], and the binding rule
  nodes ([SBR-TRUST], [SBR-STRUCTURE], [SBR-READ-VS-REASON], [SBR-DEGRADE]) — and points at the
  readable twin BY PATH, exactly as it already does for the role descriptor and the gifts. Rule
  CONTENT stays in the spec, where it is authored, linted and queryable.
- requirement: **(the structural half, and it is what makes this durable)** `SBR_FLOW_STEPS` in
  `ptycore.py` is a MIRROR of [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW]'s step names, enforced by a test that PARSES the
  step names out of the corpus. Adding a step to the spec turns the build RED until the prompt
  covers it. Without that, "we updated the prompt" is a one-off and the next amendment diverges
  silently ([INSIGHT-PARTIAL-SPEC-AMENDMENT-IS-A-FORK-GENERATOR]). This is the same mirror +
  enforcing-test shape `bootstrap_paths.py` uses ([SBR-PATHS-SINGLE-SOURCE] `enforced-by`).
- requirement: **(the manifest agreement check — this part is NEW machinery, not a restatement)** the
  prompt's manifest sections are declared as DATA, and any manifest key that is neither consumed nor
  on an explicit `DELIBERATELY_OMITTED` list (each entry carrying its reason) is NAMED in the prompt
  AND reported to the caller. It is [SBR-TRUST]'s "never silently omits" applied one layer up, to the
  prompt generator's own input.
- requirement: (amended 2026-08-11, fourth pass — INSTANCE ID WINS, and this is normative over any earlier reading) THE MANIFEST IS THE ARTIFACT SOURCE; THE INSTANCE ID IS THE IDENTITY. The signature is `build_bootstrap_prompt(manifest, *, instance_id="")` and the name the prompt uses is `ident = instance_id or manifest["id"] or "intelligence"`, threaded through `build_bootstrap_for(intelligence_id, ...)` which already had the instance id in hand. `manifest["id"]` is the FALLBACK ONLY — for a caller with no instance to name (a hand-built manifest, a preview render). A prompt that names anything other than the running instance is a defect, not a cosmetic mismatch.
- evidence: (why it matters the moment chain leg 3 works, measured 2026-08-11) `build_bootstrap_prompt` derived the peer name from `manifest["id"]` while `build_bootstrap_for` used the instance id ONLY TO RESOLVE THE PATH — two derivations of one name, in one call chain. With a ROLE manifest that means a child running as `architect-hosted`, orienting from `composeux-architect.meta.json`, would be told "You are the Intelligence peer composeux-architect" while its channel topics, its `intelligence-launcher-<id>` lock, its `self/<id>/*` telemetry and its refusal publish all say `architect-hosted`: IT WOULD POST TO A CHANNEL UNDER A NAME NOBODY TOLD IT IT HAD. The fix was verified by REVERTING the two lines and watching the new regression test fail with the wrong name — a green test that has never been seen red is not evidence.
- evidence: (why it was invisible for as long as it was) chain leg 3 had NEVER ONCE been exercised with a real ROLE manifest. Only `architect.meta.json` existed — an INSTANCE manifest whose `id` happens to equal its role — so the two derivations agreed BY ACCIDENT and no test could tell them apart. Minting the first real role manifest is what exposed it. AN AGREEMENT THAT HOLDS BY COINCIDENCE IS NOT AN AGREEMENT, AND A LEG NEVER EXERCISED IS NOT A LEG THAT WORKS: every fixture for a two-derivation agreement MUST make the two inputs DIFFER, or it tests the coincidence.
- evidence: **(why a mechanical check and not a careful list)** the builder consumed six manifest keys
  and silently ignored FIVE path-bearing ones — `principles`, `method_flow`, `bootstrap_read`,
  `toolbox`, plus the `registration` contract. So a spawned instance was never pointed at
  `bootstrap/session-bootstrap-read.md` and arrived with a role and a warm model and NO current
  state: the discover-as-you-go the boot read exists to end. `method_flow` is the proof the check
  must be mechanical — it was missed BY EYE by a reviewer cataloguing this exact defect.
- known-gap: **(SPEC AMENDMENT NEEDED BEFORE BUILD — deliberately NOT invented in code)** David asked for
  an explicit READY / NOT-READY verdict closing the boot. [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW] specifies
  `report-coverage` (step 7) and `answer-part-C` (step 8) and terminates at `begin-work-oriented`
  (step 9), but NO node requires a binary go/no-go verdict with a named blocker. The prompt therefore
  asks the instance to state whether it completed every step THROUGH `begin-work-oriented` and, if
  not, which step it is blocked at — a demand to report against the existing flow, not a new rule.
  A true READY/NOT-READY gate should be added to [SPEC:SESSION-BOOTSTRAP-READ] first, and cited here
  after.
- scenario:
  - when: a step is added to [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW]
  - then: `test_the_flow_step_mirror_matches_the_SPEC` FAILS until the prompt carries it
  - when: `build_bootstrap_prompt(role_manifest, instance_id="architect-hosted")` is called with a manifest whose `id` is `composeux-architect`
  - then: the prompt's identity line is exactly `You are the Intelligence peer "architect-hosted"` and the string `composeux-architect` never appears as the peer identity anywhere in it (TERM-C19)
  - when: the same manifest is passed with no `instance_id` at all
  - then: the prompt falls back to `manifest["id"]` — the fallback is deliberate, is the ONLY path that may use the manifest's name, and is what a preview render or a hand-built manifest gets
- verify-machine: the mirror test above; a test asserting the prompt names every step the spec names;
  a test that a NEW manifest key cannot be silently dropped; and a grep guard that the ritual spec
  path is composed from `bootstrap_paths`, never a literal.
- verify-machine: TERM-C19's exact-string assertions — the identity line for an instance id that DIFFERS from the manifest id, and the negative assertion that the role name is absent as a peer identity; a test that `build_bootstrap_for` threads its `intelligence_id` through to the prompt rather than only to the path; a grep guard that `manifest["id"]`/`manifest.get("id")` has exactly ONE read site in the prompt builder, the fallback expression itself.
- verify-human: David reads a generated prompt end to end and confirms it induces the ritual he
  ratified rather than a paraphrase of it.
- verify-human: David cold-starts a child under a role with no per-instance manifest and confirms the instance introduces itself, and answers on the channel, by its INSTANCE id — not by the role it oriented from.
- change: "2026-08-11 (fourth pass): the prompt's peer name now comes from the RUNNING INSTANCE, not from the manifest. `build_bootstrap_prompt(manifest)` became `build_bootstrap_prompt(manifest, *, instance_id=\"\")` with `ident = instance_id or manifest[\"id\"] or \"intelligence\"`, threaded through `build_bootstrap_for`; `manifest[\"id\"]` is now the FALLBACK only. Nothing about the CITE-don't-restate rule above changes — this is about the one field the prompt states rather than cites."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "`build_bootstrap_prompt(manifest)` set `ident = manifest[\"id\"]`, while `build_bootstrap_for(intelligence_id, ...)` used the instance id ONLY to resolve the manifest PATH. Two derivations of one name lived in a single call chain, and the only manifest that had ever existed was `architect.meta.json` — an INSTANCE manifest whose `id` equals its role — so the two always agreed and neither the code nor any test distinguished them."
    reasoning: "A child's name is not a property of the artifacts it reads; it is a property of the process that is running. Every other namespace already knew that — channel topics, the `intelligence-launcher-<id>` lock, `self/<id>/*`, the refusal publish all key on the instance id — so the prompt was the single dissenting derivation, and the one that speaks to the instance in the second person. Making the instance id win is not a preference between two defensible sources; it removes a second source that should never have been consulted for this question ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]'s one-id-one-instance rule, applied to the sentence that tells the child its own name)."
    rejected_alternative: "Require every role manifest to omit `id`, so `manifest[\"id\"]` cannot be wrong — rejected: `id` is a REQUIRED key and it legitimately names the ROLE in a role manifest, which is what makes leg 3 readable to a human; removing it to protect one consumer is fixing the data to suit the code. Also rejected: assert at load time that `manifest[\"id\"] == instance_id` and refuse otherwise — that forbids the role manifest outright, which is the artifact [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN] leg 3 exists to make possible."
    invalidating_assumption: "That the caller always knows the instance id at prompt-build time. Every host does today — `build_bootstrap_for` takes it as its first argument. If a future caller builds a prompt before an instance id is assigned (a pooled or lazily-named child), the fallback silently supplies the manifest's name again and the defect returns wearing the fallback's clothes; the answer then is to make `instance_id` REQUIRED and refuse, not to widen the fallback."
- upholds: [SBR-READ-VS-REASON], [SBR-TRUST], [SBR-PATHS-SINGLE-SOURCE]
- relates-to: [CXC-IC-META]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

## Changes

### [CXC-TERM-CHANGELOG] This document's own change record — one NODE per change, and why

- note: this section was untagged until 2026-08-11 (third pass). A `## Changes` heading carries
  no bracketed id, and a node body ends at the NEXT heading of ANY level — so every `change:` /
  `context_for_change:` under it was property-shaped and consumed by NOTHING. `lts_lint`
  returned zero errors over those lines; `lts_preview`'s line accounting is what named them.
- note: (why each change is its OWN node rather than repeated properties on this one) repeated
  `change:` keys on one node parse into a LIST and survive, but repeated `context_for_change:`
  mappings COLLAPSE — the parser keeps the last and drops the rest. Measured with `lts_preview`
  on the first attempt at this fix: three change entries, ONE surviving `context_for_change`.
  So one node per change, each carrying its own block. Not a style choice — the alternative
  silently loses two thirds of the reasoning while reporting zero unaccounted lines.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

#### [CXC-TERM-CHANGE-1-IDENTITY-AND-BOOTSTRAP] 2026-08-11, first glass check — the env leak and the bootstrap that never fired

- change: "2026-08-11, FIRST LIVE GLASS CHECK — two defects that each blocked the widget hosting a real session. (1) The spawned instance INHERITED its host's identity; the child env is now SANITISED ([CXC-TERM-CHILD-IDENTITY]). (2) The cold-start re-register bootstrap NEVER FIRED, anywhere; it is now DEFAULT ON in both hosts and wired into the widget ([CXC-ICL-COLD], which this spec's [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS] already said the widget 'inherits unchanged'). Plus: the claude binary resolves through $CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "`ptycore.PtyChild.start()` branched — `execvpe` with a merged `dict(os.environ)` when `env_extra` was set, a bare `execvp` otherwise — so the child got the host's environment verbatim either way. `BOOTSTRAP` defaulted OFF in `launcher.py`, was called 'experimental' in its docstring, its config table and `intelligence/launcher/README.md`, and the widget did not implement it at all (`terminal.py` mentioned it only in a comment). `main()` additionally gated it on `META_MANIFEST` being SET — which is unset by default — so even an explicit `BOOTSTRAP=1` injected nothing. The launcher's injection was `inject(prompt + \"\\n\")`, a bulk write ending in a line feed."
  - reasoning: "Both defects are the same shape: a behaviour that was specified and believed, and was structurally unable to happen. The env leak is not a launch-hygiene problem — this widget's INTENDED deployment is from inside a Claude Code session, so it inherits ALWAYS, in production, and the only fix is at the spawn. The bootstrap was ratified as the default in [CXC-ICL-COLD] and the code said the opposite in three places; a spec that says 'default on' against code that says 'experimental, default off' leaves the next reader to guess which is true. Both fixes therefore land in `composeio.ptycore`, where BOTH hosts inherit them, rather than in either host."
  - rejected_alternative: "Strip the env in the two CALLERS (launcher + widget) instead of in the core — rejected: two copies of a security-shaped list is the fork-divergence [CXC-TERM-PTYCORE] exists to prevent, and the one that drifts is the one nobody reads. Also rejected: keeping `build_bootstrap_prompt` in `launcher.py` and having the widget call it — a widget cannot import a `main()` module ([CXC-TERM-PTYCORE] known-hazard), so it would have been RE-IMPLEMENTED, and a forked ORIENTATION PROMPT is the worst possible thing to duplicate."
  - counter_argument: "Typing a prompt into the child at startup is exactly the keystroke injection [CXC-TERM-DIALOGS] bans permanently — the thing that collided with the human and hung."
  - counter_response: "[CXC-TERM-DIALOGS] draws that line itself, explicitly ('NOT implied ... this bans auto-answering DIALOGS. It does NOT ban the re-register BOOTSTRAP'), and its verify-machine already carves out 'the bootstrap timer excepted'. The distinction is mechanical and is now guarded mechanically: no detector, no output pattern-match, no keystroke at startup — the EXISTING fixed settle delay is reused rather than a readiness detector invented, and a structural test asserts `_type_bootstrap` touches nothing on the byte path. TERM-C5's 'zero bytes between spawn and the first keypress' is asserted as an ORDERED property (empty before the delay, the prompt after it) through a REAL spawn."
  - implication: "`build_bootstrap_prompt` / `load_manifest` / the BOOTSTRAP env readers moved to `composeio.ptycore` and are re-exported from `launcher.py` under their historical names. `Launcher.arm_bootstrap()` was extracted from `run()` so the arming is testable at all — `run()` takes a flock, installs signal handlers and blocks. The carry-depth-by-reference discipline moved with the builder, intact and now asserted: a test writes a real artifact and proves its CONTENTS never appear in the prompt."

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

#### [CXC-TERM-CHANGE-2-PROMPT-DERIVED] 2026-08-11, second pass — the prompt DERIVES the ritual instead of restating it

- change: "2026-08-11 (same day, second pass): the bootstrap PROMPT rebuilt to DERIVE the wake ritual from [SPEC:SESSION-BOOTSTRAP-READ] rather than restate it, plus a mechanical manifest agreement check ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]). Three defects in the prompt itself: it carried a SUBSET of the manifest, it told the instance to READ the dusk gifts, and it ended without a report."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "`build_bootstrap_prompt` consumed six manifest keys and silently ignored five path-bearing ones — most damagingly `bootstrap_read`, so no spawned instance was ever pointed at the STRATEGIC READ. Its dusk-gift line said 'read each and re-register'. It ended at 'confirm re-registration and only THEN begin work' with nothing observable. `ptycore.py` referenced [SPEC:SESSION-BOOTSTRAP-READ] ZERO times."
    reasoning: "The first draft of this fix wrote the ritual's rules INTO the prompt string — 'reason 20-30s before opening each answer', the ~10%/~40% figures, the coverage vocabulary. David caught that this creates a fork: those rules are ratified nodes ([SBR-READ-VS-REASON], [SBR-TRUST], [SBR-DEGRADE]) and a Python-string copy of them drifts silently. The correct shape is the one the prompt ALREADY used for artifacts — point BY REFERENCE — extended to the ritual itself, with a test that parses the spec so an amendment cannot be applied half-way."
    rejected_alternative: "Parse the `.lts` at prompt-BUILD time so the steps are always live — rejected: `ptycore` is stdlib-only and spawns must not depend on a corpus file being present or parseable at runtime; a boot that fails because the spec moved is worse than one carrying a mirror the CI keeps honest. The mirror + enforcing-test shape is what `bootstrap_paths.py` already established here."
    counter_argument: "Citing node ids at an instance that has not yet read the corpus is useless — it cannot resolve `[SBR-TRUST]` before it boots."
    counter_response: "It can: the prompt points at the readable twin BY PATH (`bootstrap/session-bootstrap-read.md`, the manifest's own `bootstrap_read`) AND at the normative `.lts`, and both are plain files readable before any MCP surface is up. The ids make the citation precise and greppable; the paths make it reachable. That is carry-depth-by-reference exactly as the gifts use it."
    invalidating_assumption: "That [SBR-PROCESS-FLOW] keeps its step names as backticked tokens on numbered lines — the mirror test's parse depends on that shape. If the node is restructured (e.g. into a table), the parser must be updated with it; it fails loudly rather than silently matching nothing, because the step list would come back empty and the equality assertion would fail."
    implication: "Three fixes, three different provenances, and the distinction is load-bearing: reason-don't-read and the coverage/Part-C report were ALREADY SPECIFIED and simply unbuilt (build against spec); the manifest agreement check is NEW machinery applying a ratified principle one layer up (record, then build); a true READY/NOT-READY verdict is genuinely NEW and is NOT in code — it needs a [SPEC:SESSION-BOOTSTRAP-READ] amendment first, and is recorded as a known-gap instead."

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

#### [CXC-TERM-CHANGE-3-FIRST-LIVE-SPAWN] 2026-08-11, third pass — the FIRST LIVE SPAWN and its six findings

- change: "2026-08-11 (third pass) — THE FIRST LIVE SPAWN OF A REAL `claude`. Every prior test used a `python -c` stand-in. Six findings, all ratified by David as rulings: THREE defects (one shared identity producing three symptoms, [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] + [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]; the channel silently EVICTING a live peer, [CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE] in spec 34; the bootstrap firing into a blocking dialog read, [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV]); ONE superseded design (the fixed settle delay, kept with its evidence as [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]); ONE gap (a hosted child had no context monitor while the prompt told it to watch one, [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY]); ONE scope cut (⌘V in, ⌘C out for want of a selection model, [CXC-TERM-INPUT]). Plus the general rule the census proved: [CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]."
- context_for_change:
    prior_state: "The scene hardcoded `intelligence_id: architect`, the same id as the host, so one id addressed two instances: `channel.mjs:485-490` displaced the HOST's bridge, `self/architect/*` reported the host's status, and the console labelled the host's transcript with a name that fit both. `default_meta_manifest` resolved exactly one path, `<id>.meta.json`. Bootstrap delivery was a `threading.Timer(BOOTSTRAP_DELAY_S=3.0)` typing into the PTY. `HarnessMonitor` was constructed only in `launcher.py:389`; `terminal.py` never mentioned it, while `ptycore.py:1079` told every child to watch `self/context_level`. `terminal.py:821` swallowed every Cmd-chord, so ⌘V never reached the paste path `on_text` already implements. All five census profiles ran an EMPTY MCP config with permission prompts suppressed, and none had a dialog."
    reasoning: "Three of the six are the same defect class: a mechanism that was correct under an assumption nobody re-read. The channel's displacement rule assumed same id implies same instance — true for a restart, false for a hosted child. The settle delay assumed a bounded startup — false against human-gated reads. `ptycore.py:222`'s `?2004h` claim assumed the census measured the deployed configuration — false by construction, because the safety exclusions that made the census safe also removed the dialogs. Each is cheap to see once named and invisible until a real spawn names it, which is why the FIRST LIVE SPAWN returned six findings after weeks of green stand-in tests."
    rejected_alternative: "Fix the three identity symptoms where they surfaced — teach the channel, the telemetry and the transcript labeller to disambiguate two instances sharing an id. Rejected: it is three exceptions to three correct rules, and it leaves `intelligence/<id>/*` ambiguous as an address. Also rejected: tune `BOOTSTRAP_DELAY` upward, which keeps a timer standing in for a state check and moves the failure to a slower machine."
    counter_argument: "Deleting the settle mechanism and the manifest fallback removes two things that let a marginal spawn still produce a session. A refusal helps nobody at 2am."
    counter_response: "Both removals replace a SILENT degradation with a LOUD one, and both keep an explicit opt-out — `BOOTSTRAP=0` for an intentionally unoriented child, and the child still runs unchannelled when it refuses a contested channel id. The 2am case is precisely the one the old behaviour failed: a session that looks right, is unoriented, has evicted the host from its own channel, and says so only in a log the widget discards (`terminal.py::_log` is `pass`)."
    invalidating_assumption: "That `claude`'s POSITIONAL prompt is honoured in interactive mode and survives whatever startup gates intervene. `--help` documents `claude [options] [command] [prompt]` and the argument is not qualified to `-p`, but the ORDERING against dialogs is unmeasured — the as-deployed census profile ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY]) is what measures it and TERM-C13 is the glass gate. If a positional prompt is swallowed by a gate, the answer is a tier-1 harness input or an explicit human 'orient' affordance — never the deleted timer."
    implication: "Six rulings land as spec across three files: this one, `34-composeux-intelligence-console.lts` ([CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE]) and `55-console-launches-the-intelligence.lts` ([CXC-ICL-COLD], whose BOOTSTRAP_DELAY requirement and settle-delay scenario are superseded). Two calls remain OPEN for David: [DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME] (how the distinct id is minted) and [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] (which process publishes the child's `self/*`). [DD-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV-FORM] is recorded as DECIDED."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

#### [CXC-TERM-CHANGE-4-ROLE-NAME-AND-INSTANCE-ID] 2026-08-11, fourth pass — the role gets a DERIVED name and the prompt names the INSTANCE

- change: "2026-08-11 (fourth pass) — THE FIRST REAL ROLE MANIFEST, and the two derivations it split apart. (1) A role's name is now the BASENAME OF ITS ROLE DESCRIPTOR (`.claude/agents/composeux-architect.md` -> `composeux-architect` -> `intelligence/meta/composeux-architect.meta.json`), which makes chain leg 3 mechanically derivable instead of a maintained convention ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]). (2) THE INSTANCE ID WINS over the manifest id in the bootstrap prompt ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]). (3) The ROLE manifest is recorded as a DISTINCT ARTIFACT KIND from the instance manifest, with `window_geometry` and `console` dropped, `channel.topics` neutralised to a shape, and `lifecycle` read as role DEFAULTS. [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]'s ruling is UNCHANGED; only its derivation description moved. Acceptance gains TERM-C19."
- context_for_change:
    reasoning: "The mechanism is the finding, and it generalises past this defect: CHAIN LEG 3 HAD NEVER ONCE BEEN EXERCISED WITH A REAL ROLE MANIFEST. The only manifest in the tree was an instance manifest whose `id` happens to equal its role, so the manifest-derived name and the instance-derived name agreed BY ACCIDENT — every test passed, and the disagreement was unobservable in principle rather than merely unobserved. Minting the first real role manifest is what exposed it. AN AGREEMENT THAT HOLDS BY COINCIDENCE IS NOT AN AGREEMENT, AND A LEG NEVER EXERCISED IS NOT A LEG THAT WORKS. The concrete cost had a role manifest resolved unnoticed: a child running as `architect-hosted` would have been told it is `composeux-architect` while its channel topics, its launcher lock, its `self/<id>/*` telemetry and its refusal publish all said `architect-hosted` — posting to a channel under a name nobody told it it had, which is [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]'s defect class arriving through the one surface that speaks to the instance in the second person."
    rejected_alternative: "Keep the short role name and a registry mapping role -> descriptor -> manifest — rejected: a registry is a third place to keep in sync, and drift between maintained places is what produced every defect found tonight. Also rejected: derive the role manifest by copying the instance manifest wholesale — it IS derived key-for-key with every `why` carried, but the instance-specific parts are removed or neutralised, because a role file carrying the literal `intelligence/architect/in` would hand every instance of the role the host's own topics. Also rejected: assert `manifest[\"id\"] == instance_id` and refuse — that forbids the role manifest outright, which is the artifact leg 3 exists to make possible."
    counter_argument: "Two of these three are documentation-shaped — a naming rule and a schema section — and a naming rule is exactly the kind of convention that drifts, so recording it is not obviously better than the convention it replaces."
    counter_response: "It is not recorded as a convention: the role name is DERIVED from an artifact that must exist anyway (the descriptor the manifest already points at), so there is nothing to keep in sync and the derivation can be asserted mechanically — TERM-C19's fixture and the basename assertion are both machine checks. The schema section is the single source the spec now CITES rather than restates ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]'s own cite-don't-restate rule applied to itself)."
    invalidating_assumption: "That one role descriptor file names exactly one role and that its basename is unique across the fleet, AND that the caller always has the instance id at prompt-build time. Break the first and leg 3 needs a qualified path or a role id declared inside the descriptor; break the second and the `manifest[\"id\"]` fallback silently supplies the manifest's name again — at which point `instance_id` becomes REQUIRED and the builder refuses, rather than the fallback being widened."
    implication: "Verified by REVERTING the two lines and watching the new regression test fail with the wrong name — a green test that has never been seen red is not evidence. The amendment lands in two files: this spec ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN], [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED], [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]'s derivation, TERM-C19, boundary row 23) and `intelligence/meta/meta-object.schema.md`, which now describes BOTH manifest kinds, the basename rule, the instance-only keys, and the instance-id-wins semantics."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-TERM]

---

**Version**: 0.4
**Created**: 2026-08-10
**Updated**: 2026-08-11 (fourth pass) — THE FIRST REAL ROLE MANIFEST. `intelligence/meta/composeux-architect.meta.json` was minted, chain leg 3 resolved for the first time, and two derivations of a child's name that had only ever agreed BY COINCIDENCE came apart. Three amendments, all David's settled rulings: a role's name is the BASENAME OF ITS ROLE DESCRIPTOR, so leg 3 is mechanically derivable rather than a maintained convention ([CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN]); the INSTANCE ID WINS over the manifest id in the bootstrap prompt, with `manifest["id"]` demoted to a fallback ([CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-DERIVED]); and the ROLE manifest is a DISTINCT ARTIFACT KIND whose instance-only keys are named and excluded (`intelligence/meta/meta-object.schema.md`, now describing both manifest kinds). [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID]'s ruling is UNCHANGED — only its derivation description. Acceptance gains TERM-C19 (an exact-string assertion that the prompt names the instance and never the role); the boundary matrix gains row 23; the change record gains [CXC-TERM-CHANGE-4-ROLE-NAME-AND-INSTANCE-ID]. The general rule this pass records: an agreement that holds by coincidence is not an agreement, and a leg never exercised is not a leg that works.
**Updated**: 2026-08-11 (third pass) — THE FIRST LIVE SPAWN of a real `claude` (every prior test used a `python -c` stand-in) returned six findings, all settled as David's rulings: [CXC-TERM-CHILD-ID] (one id, two instances — the root of three reported symptoms) and [CXC-TERM-MANIFEST-CHAIN] (`<id>` -> `<role>` -> REFUSE, loudly and on the glass); [CXC-IC-CHANNEL-INSTANCE] in spec 34 (refuse, do not silently evict a live peer); [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV] (the bootstrap rides ARGV; the whole startup-injection path is DELETED) with the superseded design preserved as [CXC-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-SETTLE-RETIRED]; [CXC-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY] (the launcher's EXISTING monitor, pointed at the child, thresholds 90/95 only); ⌘V through the existing paste path and ⌘C scoped out ([CXC-TERM-INPUT]); and [CXC-TERM-CENSUS-CONFIG-FIDELITY] (a census that excludes the configuration actually run measures a system nobody uses). Acceptance gains TERM-C13..C18; the boundary matrix gains rows 16-22; [CXC-TERM-SLICES] gains slice 5. The `## Changes` section became [CXC-TERM-CHANGELOG] because its properties were parsed by nothing.
**Updated**: 2026-08-10 — folded in the architect's addition: `--ax-screen-reader` added as a THIRD candidate target mode with the shared-state argument for it ([CXC-TERM-AXMODE]); V1's target is no longer asserted but CHOSEN FROM THE CENSUS across four profiles ([CXC-TERM-SCOPE], [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE]); the census gained three explicit validity conditions — cleared onboarding, steady state, a resize segment ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS]); BOTH prior probe attempts recorded as VOID and [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS] downgraded to UNCONFIRMED with no surviving evidence ([CXC-TERM-CENSUS-PRIOR]); the `/tui`-cannot-switch-with-background-work constraint recorded ([CXC-TERM-PIN]).
**Status**: Draft — awaiting the architect's ratify on [DD-TERM-TARGET-MODE] (awaits the census; GATES slices 2-4), [DD-TERM-PYTE], [DD-TERM-TAB-CAPTURE], [DD-TERM-HOST-SURFACE] (BLOCKS slice 2), [DD-TERM-55-SEQUENCING], and — new on 2026-08-11 — [DD-TERM-CHILD-ID-SCHEME] (BLOCKS slice 5a) and [DD-TERM-CHILD-TELEMETRY-HOST] (BLOCKS slice 5d). [DD-TERM-BOOTSTRAP-ARGV-FORM] is DECIDED. [CXC-TERM-HYPOTHESIS] is UNCONFIRMED with both prior measurements VOID, and gates [CXC-TERM-SLICES] slice 0.
**Intelligence**: composeux-architect / factlattice-editor, 2026-08-10
